WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.437
Some scenes in this program have been reenacted,

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:07.841
including the depiction of certain crime scene investigations.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:14.848
A teenager is missing.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.821
A police investigation exposes the deaths of dozens of boys and young men.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:26.526
All are traced to a single killer.

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.531
Photographer turns to rape and torture.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.803
Meticulously documenting his crimes on film and audio tape.

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:40.874
In a quiet suburb,

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.709
four people are found dead.

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:48.815
Police wonder about the kind of madman who could commit this crime.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:50.650
For serial killers,

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.852
one death is just the beginning.

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:54.554
Obsessed,

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:56.523
they strike again and again,

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:58.758
learning more with each crime,

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:00.226
improving,

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:01.895
perfecting.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.231
They're confident they'll never be stopped.

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:42.535
It's just after 6 o'clock on January 23,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.938
1978 in California,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:49.876
when Sacramento County police arrive at the home of Terry and David Wallen.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:55.615
They are called there by a neighbor at David's frantic request.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.153
22-year-old Terry Wallen has been brutally murdered.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:06.259
The house shows signs of an intense struggle between Wallen and her killer.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.804
She has been shot and her body slashed.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.644
A large knife wound runs the length of her torso.

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.949
Some of her internal organs have been removed.

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:36.322
Investigators are horrified by the viciousness of the crime.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:44.864
The scene becomes more gruesome when they find evidence that suggests the killer might have consumed Wallen's blood.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:53.306
Officers find a yogurt cup with bloody lip prints around the rim.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.714
Sacramento police don't know what to make of the scene,

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.050
but they know who can help them.

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.086
They call on a specialist,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.723
a man who's familiar with horrific and bizarre murder cases.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.029
His name is Russ Vorpagel.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.932
He is a special agent with the FBI.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:30.410
The brutality of the slaying makes Vorpagel fear that the murderer couldn't control himself and that Terry Wallen's death may be just the beginning of his rampage.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:34.747
There's just no need to disembowel a victim.

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:39.152
There's no need to reach inside and cut out body parts.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:46.893
There's no need to cut a four-inch piece of major artery and take it along with you.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:48.728
I mean,

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:49.329
hey,

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:52.966
we knew we had a nut and we were worried and we knew that something's going to happen.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.837
Vorpegel called a colleague at the FBI.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:05.645
Robert Ressler has tracked and caught some of the world's most dangerous killers.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.148
As a criminal profiler for the FBI,

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:12.418
he stakes out the darkest corners of a killer's mind.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:13.586
In the

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:20.793
1970s, he was one of the first to use the term serial killer to describe those who murder again and again.

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:25.398
Robert Ressler is a mind hunter.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:33.406
Wrestler struggles to understand the killer's mind by studying the crime scene.

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:39.012
Knowing how the killer thinks is essential to predicting his next move.

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:42.248
When will he strike?

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:44.550
Who will he target?

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:51.324
You have to have a emotional need to solve a crime.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:53.159
You have to want to know who the offender was.

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:54.961
You have to know what that person is,

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:55.762
what he's like,

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:57.730
what he was like before,

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:58.231
during,

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:59.065
and after the crime.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:05.471
But there is a danger in identifying too closely with those who destroy.

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:11.311
Ressler heeds the words of German philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche,

00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:16.149
who wrote that whoever fights monsters should not become a monster himself,

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.052
and that when one looks into an abyss,

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.522
the unblinking abyss stares directly back.

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:25.391
For Robert Ressler,

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.861
Nietzsche's observation is motivation and warning.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.933
Looking into the abyss to me means looking into the depth,

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:35.468
the deep crevices,

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.603
the dark crevices of man's human mind.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:40.340
And when it says the abyss looks back,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.408
to me that means I'm looking back at myself.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:46.946
I'm seeing my reflection because in every horrible killer,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.282
yourself is in there somewhere,

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:50.016
in part.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:52.352
Somewhere in your life you could have gone that same road.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:54.554
In the

00:05:55.521 --> 00:06:00.226
1970s, Ressler was among the original members of the FBI's Behavioral Science Unit,

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:05.365
the first FBI program to systematically research a killer's motivations.

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:13.373
Ressler interviewed some of America's most notorious murderers in prison to learn about their habits and attitudes.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:15.575
Today,

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:21.481
Ressler is a private consultant who works all over the world to help solve serial murders.

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:26.185
In January of 1978,

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:33.259
Ressler applied his knowledge to help Sacramento County investigators devise a psychological profile of Terry Wallen's murderer.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:36.529
It was the first step in tracking the killer.

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:42.301
Ressler began his profile by examining the crime scene details.

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:47.440
What you look for at a crime scene is the positioning of the body,

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:50.042
the way the body was left by the killer.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.279
You look at the state of the body from the standpoint of...

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.614
and being clothed or unclothed.

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:55.481
And of course,

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:58.818
you're looking for available weaponry,

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:01.587
or did the killer take a weapon to the scene,

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:01.854
use it,

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:02.422
and take it away?

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:05.925
These are all things that really start sizing up in your head.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.362
After Terry Wallen was shot,

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:12.799
the killer cut her body with knives taken from her own kitchen.

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.135
Then he left them in plain sight at the scene,

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:19.839
a detail that would prove instructive for Ressler.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:26.345
Because the greatest number of serial killers are male,

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:27.346
at the time,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.416
Vorpagel and Ressler conclude their suspect is a man.

00:07:31.384 --> 00:07:32.718
They also assume he's white,

00:07:33.085 --> 00:07:35.388
like the majority of American serial killers.

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:46.699
The brutality of the crime and the killer's apparent disregard for leaving evidence behind convince the pair their subject is mentally ill.

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:50.303
That helps them pinpoint the killer's age.

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:56.876
Ressler knows that mental illness usually begins in adolescence.

00:07:58.411 --> 00:08:04.951
It would take at least eight years for the killer to grow sick enough to perform the acts of violence he inflicted on Terry Wallen.

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:10.122
Assuming symptoms began at age 15,

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.392
Ressler figures the killer would be in his mid to late

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:13.993
20s.

00:08:15.528 --> 00:08:16.128
Any older,

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:19.465
and he would have already committed several gruesome homicides.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.268
Wrestler goes further.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:24.704
He believes the killer to be schizophrenic,

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:27.473
a mental illness characterized by delusions,

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:28.541
hallucinations,

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:30.042
and bizarre behavior.

00:08:30.042 --> 00:08:41.153
Get out of here Get out of here Get out Get out

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:55.635
The heinous nature of the crime points to full-blown psychosis,

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.404
a total loss of contact with reality.

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:03.609
Because he drinks the blood of his victims,

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:06.679
investigators call him the vampire killer.

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:10.383
From his office in Virginia,

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:15.755
Ressler works feverishly with Vorpagel in Sacramento to refine the killer's profile.

00:09:19.158 --> 00:09:19.992
What would he look like?

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:23.162
They are convinced police should look for someone thin.

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:29.735
Male schizophrenics generally eat poorly when they eat at all.

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:34.907
And Russ Vorpagel believes the murderer will pay little attention to his appearance.

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:43.616
A guy that is suffering from a major mental illness is probably going to have a deterioration in his hygiene.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.218
Probably doesn't cut his hair,

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.721
wears the same clothes for weeks at a time,

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:50.156
doesn't brush his teeth,

00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:51.290
is scroungy looking.

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:54.794
Given his bizarre behavior,

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:56.762
the killer would likely live alone.

00:09:58.464 --> 00:10:03.703
Vorpagel delivers a copy of the profile to the Sacramento police to circulate throughout the county.

00:10:07.206 --> 00:10:09.241
But no strong suspect emerges.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.346
The vampire killer remains at large.

00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:18.951
Will the profilers find him before he kills again?

00:10:24.624 --> 00:10:28.527
Three days after Terry Wallen's brutal murder in 1978,

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:30.896
less than a mile away from her house,

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.265
the vampire killer strikes again.

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:37.737
Police find the bodies of Daniel Meredith,

00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:39.138
Evelyn Miroff,

00:10:39.739 --> 00:10:40.740
and her son Jason.

00:10:44.176 --> 00:10:46.445
Missing and presumed dead is Miroff's

00:10:47.013 --> 00:10:47.747
22-month-old nephew,

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:49.048
Michael Ferriera.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:55.788
These new deaths convince Ressler and Forpegel that unless this murderer is found quickly,

00:10:56.255 --> 00:10:57.423
he will strike again.

00:10:59.191 --> 00:11:01.293
The mind hunters redouble their efforts.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:05.931
Using information gathered from this crime scene,

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:08.134
they refine their original profile.

00:11:12.772 --> 00:11:13.139
Again,

00:11:13.439 --> 00:11:15.508
the vampire killer shot his victims.

00:11:18.577 --> 00:11:18.911
Again,

00:11:19.045 --> 00:11:21.614
he used knives taken from the victim's kitchen.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.860
Crimes are found in the room and another was found outside.

00:11:41.534 --> 00:11:44.236
He doesn't seem to care that he's left the weapon behind.

00:11:52.111 --> 00:11:56.215
These details tell profilers that the killer is not planning his crimes.

00:11:57.249 --> 00:11:58.317
Although he brings a gun,

00:11:58.751 --> 00:12:02.988
he cuts the bodies with weapons he finds at the scene and then leaves them there.

00:12:06.492 --> 00:12:09.328
We have a knife that was used to mutilate the bodies.

00:12:09.328 --> 00:12:10.563
It was taken from the kitchen,

00:12:11.197 --> 00:12:12.298
used on the victim,

00:12:12.431 --> 00:12:15.034
and then discarded at the crime scene.

00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:22.208
Seeing this for a second time definitely reinforced in my mind that we were dealing with a highly disorganized killer.

00:12:23.743 --> 00:12:25.644
Organized and disorganized.

00:12:26.345 --> 00:12:29.548
These words describe a serial killer's behavior before,

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:30.249
during,

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:31.350
and after a murder.

00:12:38.858 --> 00:12:41.660
Organized serial killers carefully plot their moves.

00:12:42.394 --> 00:12:47.700
They cover their trails so thoroughly that little or no evidence remains to tie them to their crimes.

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:49.935
By contrast,

00:12:50.336 --> 00:12:52.972
disorganized serial killers act on impulse.

00:12:53.773 --> 00:12:55.007
Their behavior is random,

00:12:55.541 --> 00:12:57.743
making their next move difficult to predict.

00:13:01.046 --> 00:13:03.649
Investigators find bloodstains in the bathtub.

00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:10.055
Profilers believe the vampire killer may have bathed in a mixture of water and blood.

00:13:12.591 --> 00:13:18.097
The crimes are becoming more gruesome and the killer more deranged and more disorganized.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:28.808
Now we know darn well that your normal everyday person is not going to fill a tub with water and bathe in the blood.

00:13:30.509 --> 00:13:31.844
So here we see personality,

00:13:31.944 --> 00:13:33.045
here we see mental illness,

00:13:33.345 --> 00:13:34.914
we see mental deterioration.

00:13:36.348 --> 00:13:38.050
From the evidence at the crime scene,

00:13:38.417 --> 00:13:42.321
Vorpagel and Ressler reconstruct the grisly sequence of events.

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:48.594
And the terrifying portrait of a man they've never seen begins to emerge.

00:13:53.666 --> 00:13:59.672
The evidence reinforces the mind-hunter's belief that the vampire killer's appearance will continue to deteriorate.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.409
He likely won't have changed his clothes.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.245
Unless he is single and living alone,

00:14:06.579 --> 00:14:08.714
the killer can't possibly escape notice.

00:14:12.551 --> 00:14:13.986
Their subject is a thin,

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:15.387
disheveled loner,

00:14:15.855 --> 00:14:17.723
not more than 30 years old.

00:14:22.962 --> 00:14:29.201
Investigators are puzzled when murder victim Daniel Meredith's stolen red station wagon turns up a short distance away.

00:14:33.472 --> 00:14:34.940
Because it was found nearby,

00:14:35.574 --> 00:14:42.414
Ressler determines the vampire killer must have walked to the crime scene and then fled on foot after abandoning the car.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.818
The killer would be living,

00:14:46.218 --> 00:14:51.056
residing within a very close distance to that abandoned car.

00:14:51.423 --> 00:14:53.158
I said approximately one quarter mile.

00:14:55.294 --> 00:14:56.929
The day after the Meeroth murders,

00:14:57.229 --> 00:15:03.168
police receive an unrelated report that a dog has been shot and mutilated within blocks of the crime scene.

00:15:04.169 --> 00:15:05.137
For the mind hunters,

00:15:05.371 --> 00:15:07.039
it's the break they've been looking for.

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:09.174
They know that as children,

00:15:09.541 --> 00:15:12.311
serial killers often mutilate or kill animals.

00:15:13.545 --> 00:15:15.547
And he would go out and kill these pets,

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:17.416
shooting them with the same gun he shot

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.619
Wallen and Miroth and Meredith.

00:15:21.453 --> 00:15:21.987
Same gun.

00:15:22.521 --> 00:15:26.759
He'd shoot these pets and then he'd gut them and drink their blood.

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:30.963
As more animals are found dead,

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.398
the circle closes.

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:40.472
The mind hunters are convinced that the vampire killer lives near the slaughtered animals and his human victims.

00:15:42.574 --> 00:15:43.943
He won't have traveled far.

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:54.186
Police circulate the revised profile throughout the city in hopes that someone may have seen a disheveled or strange acting man.

00:15:55.587 --> 00:15:58.657
Officers concentrate on a quarter mile radius of the murders.

00:16:12.237 --> 00:16:15.908
As a woman in her late 20s is leaving a Sacramento shopping center,

00:16:16.308 --> 00:16:17.977
a strange man approaches her.

00:16:20.412 --> 00:16:22.348
Hearing of the vampire killer murders,

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:24.216
the woman contacts police.

00:16:27.086 --> 00:16:29.088
The man she eluded at the shopping center,

00:16:29.254 --> 00:16:30.189
she tells officers,

00:16:30.556 --> 00:16:32.257
is a former high school classmate,

00:16:32.691 --> 00:16:34.059
Richard Trenton Chase.

00:16:36.462 --> 00:16:38.163
She was shocked by his appearance.

00:16:40.132 --> 00:16:42.034
He had blood stains on his sweatshirt.

00:16:43.435 --> 00:16:45.671
His eyes seemed sunk into their sockets.

00:16:47.006 --> 00:16:49.074
A yellowed crust was around his mouth.

00:16:55.047 --> 00:16:56.181
According to the woman's description,

00:16:56.648 --> 00:16:59.351
Chase fits the profile of the vampire killer.

00:17:12.131 --> 00:17:13.232
Armed with a search warrant,

00:17:13.565 --> 00:17:15.100
officers enter Chase's home.

00:17:17.336 --> 00:17:19.271
They're unprepared for what they find.

00:17:23.342 --> 00:17:26.111
Nazi paraphernalia dominates the small apartment.

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:31.083
The place is littered with pamphlets about alien conspiracies.

00:17:34.186 --> 00:17:39.024
The chaos in the living room fits the profile of someone with an equally chaotic mind.

00:17:39.358 --> 00:17:39.658
Oh my lord.

00:17:41.293 --> 00:17:41.794
In the kitchen,

00:17:42.127 --> 00:17:44.496
police find appliances spattered with blood,

00:17:45.197 --> 00:17:48.400
as well as newspaper articles about the murder of Terry Wallen.

00:17:52.237 --> 00:17:55.207
Several dishes in the refrigerator contain human tissue.

00:17:57.309 --> 00:18:00.679
In a kitchen drawer are knives taken from Wallen's home.

00:18:04.216 --> 00:18:09.121
Investigators also find a toolbox and bloodstained rubber boots.

00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:18.497
Chase is arrested and charged with six counts of first-degree murder,

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:21.400
including the killing of Evelyn Miroff's nephew,

00:18:22.334 --> 00:18:23.735
22-month-old Michael Ferriera,

00:18:24.336 --> 00:18:27.439
whose remains are found not far from Chase's apartment.

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:37.116
The jury deliberates only a few hours before pronouncing Chase guilty on all counts.

00:18:39.418 --> 00:18:39.952
The sentence?

00:18:40.619 --> 00:18:41.920
Death in the electric chair.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.159
In 1979,

00:18:47.192 --> 00:18:49.895
Robert Ressler decided to talk to Chase in prison.

00:18:50.929 --> 00:18:51.897
The reason I interviewed him,

00:18:51.964 --> 00:18:52.531
very frankly,

00:18:52.564 --> 00:18:53.899
was to validate,

00:18:53.932 --> 00:18:57.436
to do an update and validate the profiling work that I had done,

00:18:57.436 --> 00:19:00.539
to talk to him directly after working on the case,

00:19:00.572 --> 00:19:01.907
after seeing the crime scenes,

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:04.643
after reading now the police reports,

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:05.611
and of course the...

00:19:05.777 --> 00:19:06.512
the court records,

00:19:06.678 --> 00:19:09.581
it's a way you really fine-tune your profiling.

00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:15.220
Chase tells Ressler that he had killed only to preserve his own life.

00:19:16.054 --> 00:19:16.889
He needed blood,

00:19:17.156 --> 00:19:17.756
Chase says,

00:19:18.023 --> 00:19:20.759
to survive what he called soap dish poisoning.

00:19:21.693 --> 00:19:26.131
Chase believed that a gooey residue under his soap dish proved he was dying.

00:19:26.999 --> 00:19:30.169
He claimed his blood was drying out and corroding his body.

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:31.770
His killings,

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:32.704
Chase contended,

00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:34.573
were made in self-defense.

00:19:35.474 --> 00:19:36.975
When he got down to the motivation,

00:19:36.975 --> 00:19:38.844
he said that the blood in his veins,

00:19:38.977 --> 00:19:40.512
the blood throughout his entire body,

00:19:41.146 --> 00:19:42.814
was turning to powder or dust.

00:19:43.348 --> 00:19:45.617
And that it was moving very slowly through his body,

00:19:45.717 --> 00:19:46.952
causing him to slow down,

00:19:47.052 --> 00:19:49.321
causing him to be fatigued and become very ill.

00:19:50.055 --> 00:19:51.790
And he felt this was being done,

00:19:51.823 --> 00:19:52.090
of course,

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:52.357
he said,

00:19:52.424 --> 00:19:55.961
by the influence of the unidentified flying objects and the aliens.

00:19:56.762 --> 00:20:00.832
And he said that the only way he could counter this was to get a fresh supply of human blood.

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:07.105
What drives a man like Chase to murder?

00:20:08.874 --> 00:20:15.781
One FBI study of 36 serial killers revealed that although most began life in two-parent homes,

00:20:16.281 --> 00:20:17.049
by age two,

00:20:17.516 --> 00:20:18.650
almost half the boys'

00:20:18.717 --> 00:20:20.652
fathers had abandoned their families.

00:20:21.787 --> 00:20:27.259
Nearly half of the surveyed offenders reported cold or uncaring relationships with their mothers.

00:20:28.193 --> 00:20:31.330
Twenty-six reported such relationships with their fathers.

00:20:32.631 --> 00:20:33.365
Nearly 70%

00:20:33.732 --> 00:20:36.435
of the families had histories of alcohol abuse.

00:20:37.603 --> 00:20:39.771
One-third had histories of drug abuse.

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:45.477
Almost all reported some form of childhood psychological,

00:20:45.811 --> 00:20:46.211
physical,

00:20:46.445 --> 00:20:47.512
or sexual abuse.

00:20:49.414 --> 00:20:49.948
As children,

00:20:50.482 --> 00:20:52.684
many serial killers tortured animals.

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:56.388
The majority did not graduate from high school.

00:20:57.956 --> 00:21:01.660
While the men had the required intelligence to perform skilled jobs,

00:21:02.027 --> 00:21:03.929
most had poor work histories.

00:21:05.163 --> 00:21:05.731
Only 20%,

00:21:06.665 --> 00:21:07.666
or six offenders,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.502
had ever managed to hold on to steady work.

00:21:12.004 --> 00:21:12.137
Yet,

00:21:12.304 --> 00:21:13.272
to all appearances,

00:21:13.605 --> 00:21:15.207
serial killers seem normal,

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:17.709
like your neighbor or your friend.

00:21:19.845 --> 00:21:22.247
Most of these people are like everyday people.

00:21:22.347 --> 00:21:24.650
They oftentimes are intelligent,

00:21:24.683 --> 00:21:25.651
they're attractive,

00:21:25.984 --> 00:21:27.352
they're good conversationalists,

00:21:27.753 --> 00:21:30.989
IQs usually up at least high,

00:21:31.023 --> 00:21:31.323
normal,

00:21:31.323 --> 00:21:31.790
or beyond,

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:33.659
and they're very deceptive.

00:21:35.727 --> 00:21:38.330
Ressler was opposed to the death sentence for Chase.

00:21:39.598 --> 00:21:39.898
Chase,

00:21:39.898 --> 00:21:40.432
he argued,

00:21:40.632 --> 00:21:44.736
was mentally ill and should have been locked up for life in a prison hospital.

00:21:45.804 --> 00:21:47.939
Prison psychiatrists eventually agreed.

00:21:49.841 --> 00:21:54.246
Chase's sentence was commuted to life in the California Medical Facility in Vacaville.

00:21:55.881 --> 00:21:59.451
But Chase killed himself in his prison cell just after Christmas,

00:21:59.451 --> 00:22:00.385
1980.

00:22:02.721 --> 00:22:07.025
He had stockpiled his antidepressant pills and then taken them all at once.

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:16.201
Richard Trenton Chase's brief and chaotic reign of terror had come to an end.

00:22:17.436 --> 00:22:18.170
In contrast,

00:22:18.637 --> 00:22:23.442
one of America's most cunning serial killers was also one of the most organized.

00:22:24.609 --> 00:22:27.145
Highly intelligent and a talented photographer,

00:22:27.746 --> 00:22:29.748
this murderer specialized in kidnapping,

00:22:30.282 --> 00:22:30.515
rape,

00:22:30.882 --> 00:22:31.516
and torture.

00:22:33.852 --> 00:22:36.254
Police called him the unidentified subject,

00:22:36.722 --> 00:22:37.656
or unsub.

00:22:39.891 --> 00:22:41.326
He was a master of planning.

00:22:42.728 --> 00:22:45.897
He used film and audio tape to record his crimes.

00:22:46.932 --> 00:22:48.200
In a rented storage shed,

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:53.071
he stockpiled pictures of his victims and recordings of their anguished cries.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:59.578
He was so meticulous that he would leave virtually no evidence of his atrocities,

00:23:00.212 --> 00:23:02.848
except for the photographs he took himself.

00:23:06.451 --> 00:23:10.255
Unsub customized his cars to resemble unmarked police cars.

00:23:14.292 --> 00:23:19.030
He added heavy duty suspensions and strong restraints so that once inside,

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:21.900
his victims had virtually no chance of escape.

00:23:27.672 --> 00:23:30.308
For five years he traveled from state to state,

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:34.212
never staying in one place too long.

00:23:36.948 --> 00:23:38.016
He was self-reliant,

00:23:38.517 --> 00:23:40.285
financing his crimes of kidnapping,

00:23:40.519 --> 00:23:40.752
rape,

00:23:40.886 --> 00:23:42.921
and torture with yet another crime.

00:23:44.689 --> 00:23:46.191
He was a skilled counterfeiter.

00:23:48.293 --> 00:23:51.229
He was confident that he would never be caught.

00:23:56.568 --> 00:23:57.202
In Manassas,

00:23:57.302 --> 00:23:57.736
Virginia,

00:23:58.003 --> 00:24:02.741
a team of former FBI agents pursue some of the world's most dangerous criminals.

00:24:05.277 --> 00:24:07.279
They call themselves the Academy Group.

00:24:09.581 --> 00:24:10.048
Between them,

00:24:10.315 --> 00:24:14.152
they have amassed years of experience investigating serial killings.

00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:23.862
Robert Hazelwood and Peter Smarek are experts in psychological profiling of criminals.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.265
If you in life are an impulsive person,

00:24:27.866 --> 00:24:29.034
you act without thinking,

00:24:29.668 --> 00:24:31.937
well then that crime scene may be very,

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:32.671
very sloppy.

00:24:33.238 --> 00:24:35.674
and be a reflection of your impulsive acts.

00:24:36.341 --> 00:24:37.042
On the other hand,

00:24:37.809 --> 00:24:38.844
if in life you're a cold,

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:40.178
calculating individual,

00:24:40.679 --> 00:24:42.614
you don't take any unnecessary risks.

00:24:43.215 --> 00:24:43.348
Well,

00:24:43.348 --> 00:24:46.518
that type of behavior can also be demonstrated at a crime scene.

00:24:50.322 --> 00:24:51.857
Even with crime scene evidence,

00:24:52.224 --> 00:24:54.493
categorizing serial killers is difficult.

00:24:57.095 --> 00:25:01.700
Some serial killers demonstrate a mixture of disorganized and organized habits.

00:25:03.101 --> 00:25:03.768
All too often,

00:25:05.136 --> 00:25:10.141
if an investigator starts looking at a crime scene and tries to put the offender into one of these nice,

00:25:10.308 --> 00:25:11.610
neat little categories,

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:16.915
he's going to be disappointed when the person he arrests doesn't fit these categories in all areas.

00:25:22.554 --> 00:25:24.489
In the seaside resort of Ocean City,

00:25:24.589 --> 00:25:25.090
Maryland,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.560
a young woman has just finished her shift at a convenience store.

00:25:33.298 --> 00:25:34.566
It is Memorial Day weekend,

00:25:34.566 --> 00:25:36.301
1979.

00:25:37.302 --> 00:25:40.772
She is approached by a man who identifies himself as a police officer.

00:25:41.907 --> 00:25:45.710
He shows her his identification and is driving an unmarked police car.

00:25:47.012 --> 00:25:50.382
He tells the woman she is a suspect in a series of robberies.

00:25:51.383 --> 00:25:54.986
and he insists that she accompany him to police headquarters for questioning.

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:01.359
He handcuffs her and pushes her into the car.

00:26:05.997 --> 00:26:10.902
It's the beginning of the longest and most terrifying experience of a woman's life.

00:26:14.839 --> 00:26:16.508
The man is no police officer.

00:26:19.044 --> 00:26:20.245
He is unsub.

00:26:24.149 --> 00:26:25.917
And she's his latest victim.

00:26:30.689 --> 00:26:34.626
He takes her to a deserted building where he repeatedly rapes and tortures her.

00:26:36.861 --> 00:26:39.164
He photographs and tape records the ordeal.

00:26:41.299 --> 00:26:47.973
I think one of the buzzwords today that we use is power and control.

00:26:48.974 --> 00:26:50.442
And whether you're dealing with a rapist,

00:26:50.809 --> 00:26:52.344
whether you're dealing with a serial killer,

00:26:52.777 --> 00:26:55.246
I think it still comes down to those two basic elements,

00:26:55.714 --> 00:26:59.084
power and control over the life of another individual.

00:27:03.388 --> 00:27:03.888
Eventually,

00:27:04.089 --> 00:27:06.791
he will push the dazed woman back into his car.

00:27:09.394 --> 00:27:11.262
Take her for one last ride.

00:27:15.634 --> 00:27:17.002
He stops the automobile,

00:27:18.069 --> 00:27:19.337
opens up the passenger door,

00:27:19.804 --> 00:27:21.906
and rolls the woman out into a ditch.

00:27:26.511 --> 00:27:27.712
But he did not kill her.

00:27:29.981 --> 00:27:31.549
He allowed this victim to live.

00:27:32.684 --> 00:27:33.485
And as a result,

00:27:34.185 --> 00:27:36.087
within a number of hours when daylight came,

00:27:36.221 --> 00:27:36.988
she was found,

00:27:37.122 --> 00:27:37.922
she was rescued,

00:27:38.323 --> 00:27:42.894
and now became a full-fledged FBI investigation for kidnapping.

00:27:45.997 --> 00:27:49.300
The FBI set up a massive manhunt for Unsub.

00:27:50.435 --> 00:27:54.039
The Ocean City woman was the latest in a long list of his victims.

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:56.641
Some were tortured,

00:27:57.042 --> 00:27:57.909
others murdered.

00:27:59.411 --> 00:28:02.547
but many years passed before there was a break in the case.

00:28:07.419 --> 00:28:10.255
At the same time the FBI searched for Unsub,

00:28:10.622 --> 00:28:13.525
the Secret Service was on the trail of an elusive counterfeiter,

00:28:14.192 --> 00:28:22.367
a man who routinely passed fake $20 bills to unsuspecting store clerks at malls and shops in the Midwest and on the East Coast.

00:28:23.868 --> 00:28:25.904
The one thing about any law enforcement agency,

00:28:26.371 --> 00:28:27.572
no matter how good we are,

00:28:28.106 --> 00:28:32.610
you need an element of luck and you need the cooperation of an alert citizenry.

00:28:33.678 --> 00:28:34.546
In this instance,

00:28:35.013 --> 00:28:41.653
an alert clerk recognized a composite sketch as a customer who was currently in the store.

00:28:42.654 --> 00:28:44.656
She notified mall security,

00:28:45.223 --> 00:28:47.559
mall security notified local authorities,

00:28:47.992 --> 00:28:49.594
and within a short period of time,

00:28:49.928 --> 00:28:51.663
the suspect was apprehended.

00:28:53.631 --> 00:28:57.035
The man police arrest is James Mitchell deBartolaben.

00:28:58.903 --> 00:29:03.942
The Secret Service soon discovers that deBartolaben is not just a counterfeiter.

00:29:10.281 --> 00:29:13.852
Secret Service agents search a storage unit rented by deBartolaben.

00:29:17.722 --> 00:29:20.992
Inside are sheaves of the fake currency he's been passing.

00:29:23.228 --> 00:29:23.995
But to their shock,

00:29:24.596 --> 00:29:28.933
agents also discover a grotesque treasure trove of pictures and audio tape.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:36.407
The 1983 discovery unmasks the focus of the FBI investigation.

00:29:43.982 --> 00:29:44.849
Robert Hazelwood,

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:49.120
then an FBI special agent who had written a criminal profile of unsub,

00:29:49.687 --> 00:29:52.891
reviewed the materials seized from de Barta-Laban's storage unit.

00:29:58.196 --> 00:30:01.699
Agents find thousands of photographs of de Barta-Laban's victims.

00:30:03.234 --> 00:30:08.973
Among them are photos and audio tape of the woman abducted in Ocean City in 1979.

00:30:10.842 --> 00:30:12.811
Some of the photos date back to the early

00:30:13.978 --> 00:30:14.078
1960s.

00:30:17.549 --> 00:30:19.818
They find the tools of a killer's trade.

00:30:21.352 --> 00:30:22.153
Victim's clothing,

00:30:26.591 --> 00:30:27.292
10 guns,

00:30:30.328 --> 00:30:31.629
phony police identification,

00:30:34.632 --> 00:30:35.033
journals,

00:30:39.737 --> 00:30:40.772
bloodstained lingerie,

00:30:42.373 --> 00:30:42.974
and jewelry.

00:30:46.177 --> 00:30:51.416
Seven license plates allowed de Barthelaben to roam with little fear of his car being traced.

00:30:55.386 --> 00:30:58.089
The FBI also found handwritten notes.

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:02.560
They contained codes that referred to past and future crimes.

00:31:04.329 --> 00:31:08.766
Although they have uncovered de Barta-Lehben's cache of damning images and sounds,

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:10.335
agents have a problem.

00:31:11.202 --> 00:31:16.541
The killer's face remains literally out of the frame of the pictures he's taken with the victims.

00:31:18.142 --> 00:31:25.783
Investigators will have to prove de Barta-Lehben is the man in the photographs if they are to tie him conclusively to the rapes and deaths.

00:31:26.985 --> 00:31:28.319
During his FBI career,

00:31:28.686 --> 00:31:31.489
Peter Smerek was a forensic photography expert.

00:31:33.157 --> 00:31:37.328
The Secret Service sent him a number of de Barthelaben's photos to analyze.

00:31:41.532 --> 00:31:44.636
Several show a naked torso and uncovered arms.

00:31:46.504 --> 00:31:49.040
Smerek focuses his attention on the arms.

00:31:50.408 --> 00:31:53.745
They have a distinctive pattern of small moles and freckles.

00:31:55.146 --> 00:32:05.056
Smarek asks the Secret Service to temporarily remove de Barta-Laban from his prison cell in order to photograph him in the same position as the person in the confiscated photos.

00:32:06.024 --> 00:32:07.358
The gambit pays off.

00:32:08.393 --> 00:32:13.798
When Smarek compares the photographs taken by the Secret Service to the ones found in the storage unit,

00:32:14.265 --> 00:32:16.634
he finds the patterns of freckles match.

00:32:18.036 --> 00:32:20.438
It's the proof agents desperately seek.

00:32:21.806 --> 00:32:24.208
In all the years that I've worked as a criminal profiler,

00:32:25.176 --> 00:32:34.953
I can't think of another case where the offender was such an excellent photographer that unknowingly he took his own crime scene photographs,

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:37.722
which ended up putting him in prison.

00:32:37.722 --> 00:32:40.959
In 1984,

00:32:41.259 --> 00:32:43.461
de Barta-Laban was tried and convicted.

00:32:44.195 --> 00:32:44.529
Today,

00:32:44.929 --> 00:32:46.998
he is in a federal penitentiary in Texas,

00:32:47.465 --> 00:32:49.600
sentenced to multiple life terms.

00:32:51.636 --> 00:32:55.173
DeBartolabian was one of the most dangerous criminals that ever walked the face of the earth,

00:32:55.206 --> 00:32:55.873
in my opinion.

00:32:57.041 --> 00:33:05.750
He was able to commit a number of crimes over a number of years in a variety of states and never have those crimes linked to him.

00:33:06.851 --> 00:33:08.486
That made him extremely dangerous.

00:33:09.487 --> 00:33:13.391
I think he's involved in a lot more crimes than we're aware of,

00:33:13.624 --> 00:33:13.858
yes.

00:33:16.527 --> 00:33:18.296
By controlling every variable,

00:33:18.863 --> 00:33:19.263
weapons,

00:33:19.597 --> 00:33:20.098
location,

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:20.898
and victim,

00:33:21.599 --> 00:33:24.769
the organized serial killer can slip through any dragnet,

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:29.340
getting away with murder for years as he hides in plain sight.

00:33:30.975 --> 00:33:33.277
Such was the case of John Wayne Gacy,

00:33:33.911 --> 00:33:36.014
a prominent Chicago area businessman.

00:33:37.081 --> 00:33:38.416
Well liked and respected,

00:33:39.083 --> 00:33:41.386
Gacy camouflaged a terrible secret.

00:33:43.888 --> 00:33:45.423
In 1978,

00:33:45.456 --> 00:33:46.124
in Des Plaines,

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:46.724
Illinois,

00:33:47.458 --> 00:33:49.894
15-year-old Robert Piest was reported missing.

00:33:51.062 --> 00:33:55.666
Having learned that Gacy was preparing a remodeling bid for a drugstore where Piest worked,

00:33:56.300 --> 00:34:01.239
Chief Detective Joseph Kozenzak of the Des Plaines Police Department goes to Gacy's home.

00:34:03.674 --> 00:34:05.410
Gacy denies knowing Piest.

00:34:08.479 --> 00:34:10.114
But investigators are suspicious.

00:34:11.883 --> 00:34:16.954
A background check reveals Gacy was once convicted of the sexual abuse of a teenage boy.

00:34:18.456 --> 00:34:20.892
Kozunczak places Gacy under surveillance.

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:24.162
Initially cooperative with the police,

00:34:24.662 --> 00:34:29.000
Gacy becomes increasingly surly as Kozunczak's investigation continues.

00:34:31.769 --> 00:34:33.337
He had a real mean look in his eyes.

00:34:34.005 --> 00:34:37.542
You could feel there was hatred there.

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:46.217
And that he really didn't care what we were investigating and was more concerned about trying to stay out of the way.

00:34:47.752 --> 00:34:51.289
Gacy threatens a lawsuit if police continue their surveillance.

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:53.357
Officers persist,

00:34:53.858 --> 00:34:56.094
suspecting that Gacy has much to hide.

00:34:57.228 --> 00:35:01.199
But even they are not prepared for what they'll find.

00:35:05.203 --> 00:35:07.371
For several weeks in 1978,

00:35:07.805 --> 00:35:11.542
Des Plaines police didn't let John Wayne Gacy out of their sight.

00:35:13.211 --> 00:35:13.578
Finally,

00:35:14.045 --> 00:35:16.013
it came time to search his house.

00:35:20.518 --> 00:35:24.122
The search uncovers a photo receipt that belonged to Robert Piest,

00:35:24.689 --> 00:35:26.991
linking Gacy to the boy's disappearance.

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.629
A more thorough search would reveal even more.

00:35:33.531 --> 00:35:37.034
Chief Detective Kozunczak was the first to enter Gacy's home.

00:35:38.269 --> 00:35:44.475
I was one of the first people that arrived at the house and went in through the back door,

00:35:44.575 --> 00:35:45.076
walked in.

00:35:45.676 --> 00:35:51.782
The whole place was just dark and kind of eerie.

00:35:52.316 --> 00:35:54.085
All the window blinds had been closed.

00:35:56.287 --> 00:35:57.355
And there was some sense of,

00:35:57.388 --> 00:36:00.057
I don't know,

00:36:00.091 --> 00:36:01.025
it was kind of ominous,

00:36:01.225 --> 00:36:01.526
actually.

00:36:03.294 --> 00:36:14.372
Because we were starting to believe that there could be people buried underneath Gacy's house based on the information we'd gathered.

00:36:19.944 --> 00:36:21.979
Police interviewed Gacy's ex-wife,

00:36:22.346 --> 00:36:22.780
friends,

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:24.182
and former employees.

00:36:26.517 --> 00:36:30.555
Stories came out that Gacy propositioned his workers for sexual favors,

00:36:31.155 --> 00:36:32.456
that some had disappeared,

00:36:34.458 --> 00:36:36.360
and that his house had a strange odor.

00:36:37.361 --> 00:36:40.131
One of his workers suggests they check the crawl space.

00:36:43.768 --> 00:36:45.069
After rigorous questioning,

00:36:45.536 --> 00:36:48.072
Gacy admits to detectives that four years earlier,

00:36:48.272 --> 00:36:49.640
in 1974,

00:36:50.107 --> 00:36:52.510
he killed one of his homosexual partners.

00:36:54.011 --> 00:36:56.514
Gacy claims the murder was in self-defense.

00:36:57.548 --> 00:36:59.617
He hid the man's body beneath the floor.

00:37:09.927 --> 00:37:10.127
Then,

00:37:10.595 --> 00:37:12.196
Gacy shocks investigators.

00:37:14.232 --> 00:37:18.469
He confesses that he murdered almost three dozen young men and boys,

00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:21.572
also hiding many of their bodies under the floor.

00:37:24.375 --> 00:37:27.578
The confessions trigger an intense search for the missing men.

00:37:29.146 --> 00:37:33.317
Detectives examine the crawl space in the hope of finding Gacy's victims.

00:37:34.652 --> 00:37:38.389
They uncover three decomposing bodies and parts of other bodies.

00:37:42.426 --> 00:37:44.595
But the police don't stop with the crawl space.

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:48.366
Knowing more bodies must be stashed under the house,

00:37:48.699 --> 00:37:50.534
they cut away the floor to find them.

00:38:15.159 --> 00:38:15.660
Eventually,

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:19.697
they would find bodies buried under the dining room and garage floor.

00:38:22.867 --> 00:38:27.738
Under the house is a virtual killing field of buried bones and jumbled skeletons.

00:38:30.074 --> 00:38:32.677
Police arrest Casey for the murder of Robert Peace,

00:38:33.377 --> 00:38:37.148
though the bodies they uncovered were dead too long to be the missing boy.

00:38:39.717 --> 00:38:40.084
Casey,

00:38:41.052 --> 00:38:41.185
um...

00:38:42.219 --> 00:38:48.959
He figured that the best way not to get caught for his crimes was to keep control of his victims after death.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:50.294
But when you're a psychopath,

00:38:50.528 --> 00:38:51.629
a sexual psychopath,

00:38:52.163 --> 00:38:55.466
he figured nobody would ever find those bodies down there because he was too smart.

00:38:55.533 --> 00:38:56.267
Smarter than police,

00:38:56.300 --> 00:38:57.034
smarter than everybody.

00:38:57.635 --> 00:38:59.870
By stashing his victims in his own home,

00:39:00.371 --> 00:39:02.640
Gacy maintained total control over them,

00:39:03.074 --> 00:39:03.808
even in death.

00:39:04.475 --> 00:39:08.646
and could remain confident that no one would ever find them without his knowledge.

00:39:09.647 --> 00:39:11.449
Such careful attention to detail,

00:39:11.882 --> 00:39:13.351
from planning to death,

00:39:13.984 --> 00:39:16.320
is the mark of a highly organized killer.

00:39:17.121 --> 00:39:18.255
Everything about the case,

00:39:19.223 --> 00:39:22.126
especially when viewing the home inside,

00:39:22.159 --> 00:39:24.462
would indicate that Gacy was highly methodical,

00:39:24.895 --> 00:39:25.930
very well planned,

00:39:26.230 --> 00:39:30.668
and that he had thought these crimes out in great detail prior to embarking upon them.

00:39:31.936 --> 00:39:34.739
Gacy also dumped his victims in the Des Plaines River.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:38.609
Police recovered six more bodies there.

00:39:40.177 --> 00:39:41.912
One of them was Robert Pease.

00:39:44.115 --> 00:39:44.815
When it was over,

00:39:45.182 --> 00:39:48.119
police identified the remains of 33 people.

00:39:51.122 --> 00:39:59.163
Bob was very interested in this process because the FBI was formulating within their behavioral science unit

00:40:01.799 --> 00:40:09.273
Kind of an in-depth study of people who commit a series of killings and wanting to know more about them,

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:11.041
wanting to understand them,

00:40:11.108 --> 00:40:12.276
and this was a classic,

00:40:12.943 --> 00:40:13.844
classic case.

00:40:20.484 --> 00:40:26.056
The goal of the FBI's Behavioral Science Unit is to map the mind of the serial killer.

00:40:27.525 --> 00:40:35.399
So many people believe that it's the sexual act itself that he's performing with the victim that is of primary importance to him.

00:40:35.833 --> 00:40:36.534
In reality,

00:40:36.801 --> 00:40:39.270
it's the entire game that's important to them.

00:40:39.703 --> 00:40:41.005
The selection process,

00:40:41.205 --> 00:40:42.173
the surveillance.

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:47.478
knowing that I have selected you to become my victim and you don't know it.

00:40:47.945 --> 00:40:52.550
And then all of the planning that goes into the stages of actually conducting the abduction.

00:40:53.184 --> 00:41:04.462
And so they are rehearsing this and living this maybe for weeks or months before the actual abduction occurs and to a certain extent the sexual activity afterwards may almost be an aftermath.

00:41:08.032 --> 00:41:08.732
With each killing,

00:41:09.099 --> 00:41:10.634
Gacy refined his methods,

00:41:11.235 --> 00:41:12.403
learned from his mistakes,

00:41:12.703 --> 00:41:14.405
and improved on his successes.

00:41:17.408 --> 00:41:20.578
He carefully lured his victims into a well-designed trap,

00:41:21.745 --> 00:41:23.214
distracting them with weightlifting,

00:41:24.381 --> 00:41:25.616
plying them with alcohol,

00:41:26.083 --> 00:41:27.785
and showing pornographic movies.

00:41:29.487 --> 00:41:33.190
Bring it down to your chest.

00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:33.724
All the way down.

00:41:33.724 --> 00:41:33.824
Okay,

00:41:33.824 --> 00:41:34.191
can you bring it up?

00:41:34.191 --> 00:41:34.725
You can lift it up.

00:41:35.125 --> 00:41:39.964
He eventually grew so confident that he brazenly targeted a boy from his own neighborhood.

00:41:41.465 --> 00:41:45.769
Many victims came to Gacy's home hoping to get a job with his contracting firm.

00:41:47.438 --> 00:41:49.173
He'd meet with them in his garage.

00:41:52.376 --> 00:41:56.814
The soundproof garage was designed by Gacy and constructed by his company.

00:42:01.952 --> 00:42:05.289
A motorized door was the only way in or out.

00:42:07.391 --> 00:42:08.659
If his victim didn't object,

00:42:09.126 --> 00:42:12.530
Gacy would demonstrate a magic trick using trick handcuffs.

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:12.663
Like that.

00:42:12.663 --> 00:42:14.832
Like this.

00:42:15.332 --> 00:42:15.666
Real good,

00:42:15.699 --> 00:42:15.933
right?

00:42:15.933 --> 00:42:18.235
Okay.

00:42:18.235 --> 00:42:20.237
Ready?

00:42:20.237 --> 00:42:21.805
After showing how the handcuffs work,

00:42:22.306 --> 00:42:24.275
Gacy would persuade his victim to try them.

00:42:33.384 --> 00:42:33.617
All right.

00:42:33.617 --> 00:42:33.717
Now,

00:42:33.717 --> 00:42:34.618
you did it the easy way.

00:42:35.286 --> 00:42:38.188
Then he would challenge him to attempt the trick behind his back.

00:42:39.223 --> 00:42:40.357
If the victim agreed,

00:42:40.691 --> 00:42:41.792
the trap was sprung.

00:42:44.728 --> 00:42:47.498
Gacy would switch the trick handcuffs for real ones.

00:42:47.498 --> 00:42:47.665
Get on.

00:42:47.665 --> 00:42:50.334
I guess I'll light this.

00:42:50.401 --> 00:42:53.404
There you go.

00:42:53.437 --> 00:42:54.371
Once immobilized,

00:42:54.605 --> 00:42:56.407
the victim was at Gacy's mercy.

00:43:00.844 --> 00:43:00.945
Hey,

00:43:00.945 --> 00:43:03.013
come on man.

00:43:03.013 --> 00:43:03.080
One,

00:43:03.113 --> 00:43:03.247
two,

00:43:03.314 --> 00:43:03.447
three,

00:43:03.547 --> 00:43:04.882
try to twist it.

00:43:04.982 --> 00:43:08.018
Only Gacy knows exactly what happened next.

00:43:08.052 --> 00:43:09.086
You have to relax.

00:43:09.086 --> 00:43:09.386
Come on Bobby,

00:43:09.386 --> 00:43:09.820
relax.

00:43:23.667 --> 00:43:27.071
Like serial rapist and killer James Mitchell de Barta-Laban,

00:43:27.738 --> 00:43:30.207
Gacy thought he had completely covered his tracks.

00:43:31.442 --> 00:43:35.646
But mementos found during the search of Gacy's home provided key evidence,

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.082
though police didn't know it at the time.

00:43:42.653 --> 00:43:44.755
Detectives discovered driver's licenses,

00:43:45.055 --> 00:43:45.756
belt buckles,

00:43:46.090 --> 00:43:46.490
rings,

00:43:46.690 --> 00:43:47.992
and other personal effects.

00:43:50.094 --> 00:43:53.364
Ressler alerted investigators to the importance of these objects.

00:43:55.132 --> 00:43:57.067
We had actually found these items,

00:43:57.935 --> 00:44:00.337
not fully realizing what we had found.

00:44:01.138 --> 00:44:08.112
Until after Bob came in and started pointing out to us that to you a belt buckle is not important,

00:44:08.946 --> 00:44:10.981
but to the person who committed the crime,

00:44:11.649 --> 00:44:12.816
it's very important.

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:19.023
It's a visual process to help relive the crime at any given time that he wants to do so.

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:25.829
The souvenirs are a reminder of his past murders and an inspiration to kill again.

00:44:27.064 --> 00:44:27.598
To Gacy,

00:44:27.665 --> 00:44:31.835
this is some sort of a symbol of his success,

00:44:32.302 --> 00:44:33.504
a reminder.

00:44:33.804 --> 00:44:37.241
It enhances his fantasy and carries him on to the next victim.

00:44:37.741 --> 00:44:38.142
Trophies,

00:44:38.208 --> 00:44:38.676
I call them,

00:44:38.676 --> 00:44:39.543
for the serial killer,

00:44:40.377 --> 00:44:42.646
where they kind of enhances their fantasy.

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:43.981
They have these objects around.

00:44:46.517 --> 00:44:54.692
The most disturbing object found in Gacy's house was a map indicating job sites around the United States where he had worked as a contractor.

00:44:57.327 --> 00:45:01.432
Ressler believes it's likely that Gacy wouldn't have confined his killings to Chicago.

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:06.003
It only seemed logical that if Gacy's traveling,

00:45:06.036 --> 00:45:07.171
doing this in Chicago,

00:45:07.171 --> 00:45:08.305
he's doing it elsewhere as well.

00:45:08.338 --> 00:45:13.911
That's why I firmly believe that John Gacy killed more than the 33 he was convicted of.

00:45:13.977 --> 00:45:16.847
I think probably maybe twice that number.

00:45:19.216 --> 00:45:21.752
Forensic teams dismantled Gacy's house.

00:45:22.352 --> 00:45:22.720
In all,

00:45:22.953 --> 00:45:25.022
they recovered 27 bodies there.

00:45:27.157 --> 00:45:28.759
How many more Gacy killed,

00:45:29.059 --> 00:45:30.294
police would never know.

00:45:34.565 --> 00:45:35.365
For 10 years,

00:45:35.566 --> 00:45:37.601
starting in 1979,

00:45:37.601 --> 00:45:39.436
Ressler interviewed Gacy in prison.

00:45:40.571 --> 00:45:45.442
Ressler was surprised to learn that he had grown up four blocks from the killer in suburban Chicago.

00:45:47.277 --> 00:45:50.681
Gacy even remembered delivering groceries to the Ressler family,

00:45:51.215 --> 00:45:55.152
recalling in detail the distinctive flower pots that Ressler's mother used.

00:45:56.353 --> 00:45:58.622
Gacy also had darker recollections.

00:45:59.389 --> 00:46:02.659
He described for Ressler a turbulent relationship with his father.

00:46:03.727 --> 00:46:05.329
Ressler wasn't surprised.

00:46:06.263 --> 00:46:10.234
Nearly all the serial killers he knows of tell similar stories.

00:46:10.634 --> 00:46:11.435
And it's all documented.

00:46:11.435 --> 00:46:12.369
I ran away from home,

00:46:12.436 --> 00:46:12.870
as you know,

00:46:13.737 --> 00:46:14.872
when I was 19 years old,

00:46:14.905 --> 00:46:16.306
because I couldn't get along with my father.

00:46:17.307 --> 00:46:17.541
I mean,

00:46:17.541 --> 00:46:18.575
he was just overbearing.

00:46:18.876 --> 00:46:21.111
I was dumb and stupid and never would amount to anything.

00:46:21.211 --> 00:46:22.613
And so I just took off,

00:46:22.679 --> 00:46:23.013
and I said,

00:46:23.013 --> 00:46:23.514
to hell with it.

00:46:23.981 --> 00:46:25.082
I was gone for three months.

00:46:27.317 --> 00:46:27.885
From prison,

00:46:28.285 --> 00:46:30.621
Gacy made a painting and sent it to Ressler.

00:46:34.224 --> 00:46:36.827
It depicted Gacy dressed in a clown costume.

00:46:38.495 --> 00:46:39.730
When I got into clown makeup,

00:46:39.830 --> 00:46:41.098
I regressed into childhood.

00:46:41.198 --> 00:46:47.004
It was fun being a clown because you could be yourself or just let yourself go and act a fool.

00:46:47.604 --> 00:46:48.205
You know how people,

00:46:49.106 --> 00:46:49.940
if you're a businessman,

00:46:50.274 --> 00:46:52.609
you've got to keep a certain rapport and stuff like that,

00:46:52.609 --> 00:46:54.311
and everybody looks for you for an image.

00:46:55.412 --> 00:46:57.247
But as a clown with the makeup on,

00:46:57.481 --> 00:47:00.250
you could be slapstick and funny and have a good time.

00:47:00.851 --> 00:47:02.352
That's why I always enjoyed clowning.

00:47:03.387 --> 00:47:04.454
In Gacy's painting,

00:47:04.721 --> 00:47:07.057
he stands in a grove of evergreen trees,

00:47:07.457 --> 00:47:08.826
surrounded by balloons.

00:47:10.294 --> 00:47:11.895
The inscription on the back reads,

00:47:12.329 --> 00:47:16.767
You cannot hope to enjoy the harvest without first laboring in the fields.

00:47:18.235 --> 00:47:23.207
Gacy refused to explain the painting's meaning to Ressler or to anyone else.

00:47:24.474 --> 00:47:28.445
Ressler believes the painting hints at many more murders Gacy committed.

00:47:29.913 --> 00:47:30.247
He said,

00:47:30.380 --> 00:47:33.517
you can get away with a lot of things when you're behaving as a clown,

00:47:33.851 --> 00:47:35.552
because people see you as something funny,

00:47:35.619 --> 00:47:36.854
they see you as something amusing,

00:47:37.087 --> 00:47:38.956
they don't know what's beneath the grease paint,

00:47:38.956 --> 00:47:40.624
they don't know what's beneath the cloth.

00:47:40.991 --> 00:47:41.258
He said,

00:47:41.291 --> 00:47:42.492
clowns can get away with murder.

00:47:44.728 --> 00:47:48.498
John Wayne Gacy died by lethal injection on May 10,

00:47:48.498 --> 00:47:51.435
1994.

00:47:53.403 --> 00:47:56.173
Since the early years of criminal profiling in the

00:47:57.107 --> 00:48:00.577
1970s, other serial murderers have been identified and caught.

00:48:01.778 --> 00:48:03.580
Such names as Son of Sam,

00:48:05.215 --> 00:48:05.816
Ted Bundy,

00:48:06.617 --> 00:48:14.224
Jeffrey Dahmer have become known to mind hunters and to a public alternately fascinated and repelled by their crimes.

00:48:15.058 --> 00:48:17.160
Serial killers are driven by control,

00:48:17.628 --> 00:48:18.528
by dominance,

00:48:18.629 --> 00:48:19.429
by discipline,

00:48:19.529 --> 00:48:21.465
by authority.

00:48:21.865 --> 00:48:27.638
They have very warped concepts of interaction with another human being.

00:48:27.971 --> 00:48:30.007
They don't look at another human being as a human being,

00:48:30.007 --> 00:48:31.208
they look at them as an object.

00:48:33.243 --> 00:48:35.279
Robert Ressler believes that serial killing,

00:48:35.679 --> 00:48:37.514
while not limited to the United States,

00:48:37.981 --> 00:48:41.218
has intensified in this country since the end of World War II.

00:48:42.252 --> 00:48:47.190
Part of the reason has to do with a culture that glamorizes and encourages violence.

00:48:49.159 --> 00:48:50.294
Culture produces,

00:48:50.527 --> 00:48:50.827
you know,

00:48:51.228 --> 00:48:52.529
produces violent crime.

00:48:52.863 --> 00:48:54.064
I would say 75%

00:48:54.097 --> 00:48:55.666
of the world's serial killers are

00:48:56.633 --> 00:48:57.501
American products.

00:49:01.505 --> 00:49:02.606
It sounds very strange,

00:49:02.639 --> 00:49:03.307
but it's one of our,

00:49:03.674 --> 00:49:03.874
you know,

00:49:03.874 --> 00:49:04.541
it's like America,

00:49:04.741 --> 00:49:04.942
you know,

00:49:04.942 --> 00:49:06.076
it's like baseball,

00:49:06.643 --> 00:49:08.679
apple pie mom and serial homicide.

00:49:08.712 --> 00:49:14.184
It's unfortunate and it's certainly tragic in a sense,

00:49:14.217 --> 00:49:17.220
but it's one thing in the world of criminology,

00:49:17.254 --> 00:49:18.221
the United States is...

00:49:19.823 --> 00:49:22.225
Absolutely broken all records.

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:29.099
Profilers must take care that the poisonous thoughts of those they track don't pollute their own.

00:49:30.167 --> 00:49:32.069
For one who stares into the abyss,

00:49:32.469 --> 00:49:34.204
there is a danger of falling in.

00:49:34.771 --> 00:49:41.278
And the wrestler says that mind hunters must work their way back from the brink every day of their lives.

00:49:42.112 --> 00:49:43.146
I've maintained a balance,

00:49:43.447 --> 00:49:43.780
I think,

00:49:44.081 --> 00:49:49.586
consciously by isolating myself from the horror of what what I see at.

00:49:50.287 --> 00:49:51.121
I go to work at eight,

00:49:51.922 --> 00:49:52.889
I quit at five,

00:49:53.690 --> 00:49:55.292
I go back to a normal life,

00:49:55.492 --> 00:49:56.626
I have a drink,

00:49:56.693 --> 00:49:57.894
I watch the news,

00:49:58.595 --> 00:49:59.429
I cut the grass.

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:01.431
I've kept a perspective,

00:50:01.465 --> 00:50:04.968
I've kept in tune with the mainstream and the normal people of society.

00:50:06.169 --> 00:50:06.737
For Ressler,

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:10.407
profiling is less about drama than about analysis.

00:50:12.042 --> 00:50:12.709
Observe,

00:50:13.577 --> 00:50:13.877
think,

00:50:14.845 --> 00:50:15.579
conclude.

00:50:16.813 --> 00:50:20.250
and realize there are no easy or quick answers.

00:50:21.618 --> 00:50:24.621
The movie concept is that the profiler will solve the case,

00:50:24.654 --> 00:50:26.556
everybody can just fold up their tents and go home.

00:50:27.257 --> 00:50:32.629
Profiling is one tool in the overall toolbox of the investigator.

00:50:32.662 --> 00:50:34.331
It gives him one more dimension,

00:50:35.165 --> 00:50:36.433
one more piece of that puzzle.

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:38.201
And when you put it all together,

00:50:38.268 --> 00:50:39.469
sometimes it's very effective.

00:50:40.404 --> 00:50:43.373
Profilers explore the mine's darkest regions,

00:50:44.107 --> 00:50:46.443
dragging what's hidden into the light of day.

00:50:47.544 --> 00:50:50.480
Only then can it be examined and learned from.

00:50:51.882 --> 00:50:53.216
The value of profiling,

00:50:53.316 --> 00:50:54.885
as in this case of Richard Chase,

00:50:55.419 --> 00:50:57.254
is to give the police some concept,

00:50:57.320 --> 00:51:02.459
some psychological portrait or an image of the logical type of subject that they're looking for,

00:51:02.759 --> 00:51:04.094
to give them a clear picture,

00:51:04.127 --> 00:51:04.795
because otherwise,

00:51:04.828 --> 00:51:06.363
it's just an empty room.

00:51:08.865 --> 00:51:11.902
These human monsters feel they can never be caught,

00:51:12.569 --> 00:51:13.737
that they are invulnerable.

00:51:14.838 --> 00:51:15.005
Yet,

00:51:15.338 --> 00:51:16.373
with each new victim,

00:51:16.973 --> 00:51:18.108
each drop of blood,

00:51:18.875 --> 00:51:22.446
each fiber or DNA strand left at the scene of a crime,

00:51:23.013 --> 00:51:26.416
the mind hunters probe deeper into the killer's mind.

00:51:29.486 --> 00:51:32.322
Perhaps the day will come when profilers won't be needed.

00:51:33.390 --> 00:51:34.091
But for now,

00:51:34.724 --> 00:51:38.862
the Mind Hunters will have to continue looking into the abyss.

00:51:45.669 --> 00:51:46.870
In a church parking lot,

00:51:47.104 --> 00:51:48.572
a woman is found murdered.

00:51:49.639 --> 00:51:52.476
The killer leaves few clues and no weapon.

00:51:55.445 --> 00:51:58.682
Routine suicide investigation takes a macabre twist.

00:52:01.785 --> 00:52:03.987
Can a camera prove this was murder?

00:52:07.824 --> 00:52:10.060
And a fire rages in a nursing home,

00:52:10.494 --> 00:52:12.062
killing 15 residents.

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:15.332
Armed only with his photographs,

00:52:15.699 --> 00:52:20.570
it will take the coroner six years to prove this tragedy was no accident.

00:52:24.107 --> 00:52:30.080
Forensic photographers solve these crimes by preserving crucial details missed by the naked eye.

00:52:32.649 --> 00:52:34.184
After the evidence is erased,

00:52:35.118 --> 00:52:38.688
the new detectives must rely on camera clues.

00:53:11.555 --> 00:53:12.455
In Fairfax County,

00:53:12.522 --> 00:53:12.989
Virginia,

00:53:13.190 --> 00:53:14.758
in March 1989,

00:53:15.091 --> 00:53:19.663
a man called police to report a woman had fallen in an icy church parking lot.

00:53:22.032 --> 00:53:24.034
An officer reported back from the scene.

00:53:24.568 --> 00:53:26.369
The woman didn't slip and fall.

00:53:26.937 --> 00:53:27.771
She was murdered.

00:53:29.072 --> 00:53:30.140
At police headquarters,

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:34.211
forensic photographer Jeff Miller knew he had a long night ahead of him.

00:53:35.645 --> 00:53:39.916
He packed his equipment and got ready to meet investigators gathering at the scene.

00:53:43.420 --> 00:53:45.155
Where some officers shoot criminals,

00:53:45.655 --> 00:53:46.923
Miller shoots film.

00:53:49.025 --> 00:53:51.494
In his 18 years with the Fairfax police,

00:53:52.162 --> 00:53:55.131
he has witnessed countless crime scenes through his lens.

00:53:57.834 --> 00:53:59.903
If he is the eyes of law enforcement,

00:54:00.303 --> 00:54:02.839
then his photos are its long-term memory.

00:54:03.173 --> 00:54:05.442
This is the ID we got off the victim.

00:54:06.943 --> 00:54:10.146
Investigators arrived at the parking lot and planned their strategy.

00:54:11.348 --> 00:54:13.950
Detective Ed Guckenberger headed the investigation.

00:54:15.652 --> 00:54:17.320
As his team uncovered clues,

00:54:17.621 --> 00:54:18.722
they depended on Miller.

00:54:20.957 --> 00:54:24.494
It's in Miller's camera that all the clues come together,

00:54:25.595 --> 00:54:29.266
to solve a case and hopefully to catch a killer.

00:54:29.799 --> 00:54:31.568
We want to be able to document the scene,

00:54:32.469 --> 00:54:32.969
in essence,

00:54:33.036 --> 00:54:37.207
take the scene from here to the courthouse just exactly the same way that we find it.

00:54:39.809 --> 00:54:42.112
Miller began by mapping the crime scene.

00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:46.716
The initial clues were both simple and brutal.

00:54:48.118 --> 00:54:51.087
The victim was lying face up in a pool of her own blood.

00:54:51.755 --> 00:54:55.258
She had been shot several times with the fatal bullet to her head.

00:54:56.960 --> 00:54:57.460
Nearby,

00:54:57.761 --> 00:54:59.496
her purse was found untouched.

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:02.399
Robbery was not the motive.

00:55:04.434 --> 00:55:07.370
Miller first documented the wide view of the crime scene,

00:55:07.837 --> 00:55:09.272
which was blanketed in darkness.

00:55:10.173 --> 00:55:11.174
To bring it into focus,

00:55:11.508 --> 00:55:14.311
Miller used a technique called painting with light.

00:55:16.079 --> 00:55:17.981
The camera was mounted on a tripod.

00:55:18.415 --> 00:55:21.318
Its shutter kept open as an assistant covered the lens.

00:55:22.852 --> 00:55:28.124
The same frame of film was exposed repeatedly as Miller passed along the edges of the scene.

00:55:28.158 --> 00:55:28.591
They're ready.

00:55:28.925 --> 00:55:32.729
firing his flash as many as 24 times for a single picture.

00:55:33.897 --> 00:55:36.533
Each flash illuminates a slightly different area.

00:55:39.602 --> 00:55:42.272
This photograph is the result of Miller's work.

00:55:44.574 --> 00:55:46.509
The bright lights look like street lamps,

00:55:46.943 --> 00:55:48.678
but they were made by Miller's flash.

00:55:49.479 --> 00:55:51.514
Though he was holding the flash as it fired,

00:55:52.349 --> 00:55:55.185
Miller doesn't appear because he was behind the light.

00:55:56.419 --> 00:55:59.489
He remained invisible as he exposed the evidence.

00:56:02.459 --> 00:56:05.428
After Miller took his wide shot by painting with light,

00:56:05.962 --> 00:56:08.798
he moved in to photograph every detail of the scene.

00:56:11.401 --> 00:56:13.937
He worked his way closer and closer to the victim,

00:56:14.771 --> 00:56:17.640
documenting her wounds and the position of her body.

00:56:29.085 --> 00:56:30.353
You guys see these bloody tire tracks?

00:56:30.353 --> 00:56:33.356
Miller discovered some bloody tire tracks near the victim.

00:56:36.893 --> 00:56:38.762
Officers were sent to follow the trail,

00:56:39.429 --> 00:56:42.832
while others stayed behind to comb for additional clues.

00:56:45.668 --> 00:56:47.570
Miller found two bullet casings.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:53.109
The man who called the police parked his van over them.

00:56:59.582 --> 00:57:01.618
Miller photographed the evidence where it lay.

00:57:03.486 --> 00:57:07.157
Moving the van again would only have disturbed more of the crime scene.

00:57:07.157 --> 00:57:11.428
Hey guys.

00:57:11.428 --> 00:57:13.263
As Miller hunted for additional evidence,

00:57:13.730 --> 00:57:16.199
officers returned from their search of the neighborhood.

00:57:17.967 --> 00:57:20.303
They found an abandoned car a block away.

00:57:22.338 --> 00:57:23.406
They ran the tags.

00:57:23.907 --> 00:57:26.042
It was registered to the victim's address.

00:57:28.812 --> 00:57:31.614
The sight of the car became a second crime scene.

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:34.417
Again,

00:57:34.751 --> 00:57:37.120
Miller photographed the scene from every angle,

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:42.926
hoping to uncover any clues that might explain how the woman ended up so far from her vehicle.

00:57:46.396 --> 00:57:47.864
As he did at the church parking lot,

00:57:48.264 --> 00:57:50.567
Miller first photographed the site at a distance,

00:57:51.201 --> 00:57:52.902
then moved in for close-ups.

00:57:54.404 --> 00:57:56.272
The driver's side window was shattered.

00:57:58.041 --> 00:58:00.877
Pellets of glass were strewn about the car's interior.

00:58:04.214 --> 00:58:08.218
A spent cartridge lay in the snow outside the driver's side door,

00:58:09.886 --> 00:58:12.288
and a bullet was lodged in the plastic molding.

00:58:16.392 --> 00:58:17.527
On the passenger side,

00:58:17.794 --> 00:58:19.195
Miller found even more.

00:58:21.397 --> 00:58:23.933
Pressed into the thin snow were footprints,

00:58:24.501 --> 00:58:26.102
too big to be the victims.

00:58:28.805 --> 00:58:33.042
Forensic technicians have a tough time making casts of snowy tracks,

00:58:33.476 --> 00:58:36.312
and Miller knew the prints would melt with the rising sun.

00:58:38.815 --> 00:58:43.453
So it was up to him and his camera to preserve the evidence before it faded away.

00:58:45.288 --> 00:58:50.693
You can cut it out if it's in ice and try to keep it at a certain temperature and everything else.

00:58:50.727 --> 00:58:50.894
Well,

00:58:52.962 --> 00:58:54.097
I guess sometimes it works,

00:58:54.130 --> 00:58:55.031
sometimes it doesn't.

00:58:55.965 --> 00:59:02.405
There are different chemicals that you could possibly spray on it to try and keep it.

00:59:04.574 --> 00:59:06.543
When all in all is said and done,

00:59:06.876 --> 00:59:15.718
the photograph is the only evidence that was able to totally keep that impression intact.

00:59:19.155 --> 00:59:22.492
The search of the neighborhood uncovered more potential evidence.

00:59:25.862 --> 00:59:32.735
A patrolman found a second car abandoned a half mile from the church parking lot after apparently skidding off the road.

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:42.445
The second car became the third crime scene during this night of sporadic clues.

00:59:44.514 --> 00:59:46.449
Police began to search for a connection.

00:59:49.519 --> 00:59:50.086
On the seat,

00:59:50.386 --> 00:59:52.589
an officer found a leather gun holster.

00:59:55.158 --> 00:59:56.960
He ran the tags on the vehicle.

00:59:58.294 --> 01:00:00.863
The owner lived just two doors from the victim.

01:00:04.233 --> 01:00:04.734
At dawn,

01:00:05.201 --> 01:00:07.737
Jeff Miller inspected the church parking lot again.

01:00:09.005 --> 01:00:11.541
He hoped the sun would shed new light on the case.

01:00:12.408 --> 01:00:12.842
By now,

01:00:13.176 --> 01:00:14.243
the snow had melted.

01:00:15.044 --> 01:00:20.617
We wanted to come back and check the asphalt parking lot and make sure that we collected all the evidence.

01:00:20.617 --> 01:00:22.952
We don't want to leave anything behind once we leave here.

01:00:22.986 --> 01:00:23.720
You know,

01:00:23.720 --> 01:00:24.687
you can't come back.

01:00:27.757 --> 01:00:28.858
His effort paid off.

01:00:30.026 --> 01:00:34.097
Daylight revealed fragments of glass buried in the snow the night before.

01:00:36.733 --> 01:00:39.769
If the glass matched the broken window in the victim's car,

01:00:40.269 --> 01:00:43.806
police could say the car was in the parking lot when she was killed.

01:00:46.609 --> 01:00:47.644
There's something over here.

01:00:52.682 --> 01:00:55.785
The melting snow also revealed a bullet at the edge of the lot.

01:00:57.487 --> 01:00:58.021
So I'll tell you what,

01:00:58.021 --> 01:00:59.489
let me get a couple pictures of it first.

01:01:00.790 --> 01:01:04.127
Tests would determine if it matched the bullet found in the victim's car.

01:01:14.904 --> 01:01:16.139
When Miller checked the parking lot,

01:01:16.539 --> 01:01:18.074
Ed Guckenberger hit the road.

01:01:18.941 --> 01:01:19.676
His first stop,

01:01:20.143 --> 01:01:21.210
the victim's house.

01:01:22.211 --> 01:01:23.279
In homicide cases,

01:01:23.579 --> 01:01:26.416
family members are usually the first suspects.

01:01:27.517 --> 01:01:29.118
I have to read very quickly,

01:01:29.285 --> 01:01:30.586
once I knock on that door,

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:34.223
the type of response that I get from that person who opens it.

01:01:35.525 --> 01:01:36.459
And in this case,

01:01:37.460 --> 01:01:41.831
I had a husband that was dressed in a bathroom that

01:01:42.832 --> 01:01:45.201
I had woken up out of his sleep.

01:01:46.235 --> 01:01:49.472
We stated that his wife should have been home,

01:01:49.472 --> 01:01:51.240
and she was at a church meeting.

01:01:56.079 --> 01:01:57.447
As a homicide investigator,

01:01:57.480 --> 01:02:03.386
what you're going to have to do is you have to read that person because he seems sincere.

01:02:03.519 --> 01:02:06.022
Is this some ploy or something else?

01:02:06.355 --> 01:02:06.522
Well,

01:02:06.522 --> 01:02:13.830
it was nothing from what he was telling me to show that he was in any way involved in this.

01:02:16.532 --> 01:02:19.535
Confident the husband wasn't involved with the woman's murder,

01:02:20.002 --> 01:02:21.704
officers followed their next lead.

01:02:22.972 --> 01:02:24.307
They didn't have far to go.

01:02:26.676 --> 01:02:29.779
The owner of the second car was the victim's neighbor.

01:02:30.580 --> 01:02:33.316
He was also no stranger to police.

01:02:36.519 --> 01:02:40.123
The churchyard murder investigation was less than 10 hours old.

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:41.424
So far,

01:02:41.691 --> 01:02:43.960
Detective Guckenberger had no suspects.

01:02:44.627 --> 01:02:47.463
His only lead was the owner of the second car.

01:02:49.298 --> 01:02:52.902
The man police wanted to talk with was Gary Asa Donahue.

01:02:53.903 --> 01:02:56.973
Ed Guckenberger asked him to come to the station for questioning.

01:02:58.908 --> 01:03:00.843
To his buddies in his motorcycle gang,

01:03:01.144 --> 01:03:02.912
Donahue was known as Redneck.

01:03:04.547 --> 01:03:06.783
Police knew him by his criminal record.

01:03:08.584 --> 01:03:11.521
Donahue told police he'd been out drinking the night before.

01:03:12.355 --> 01:03:13.089
When he got home,

01:03:13.356 --> 01:03:15.057
he realized his dog was loose,

01:03:15.291 --> 01:03:16.793
so he drove around to look for him.

01:03:17.860 --> 01:03:21.864
He lost control of his car on the icy road and skidded into a ditch.

01:03:34.110 --> 01:03:35.044
Unable to get free,

01:03:35.511 --> 01:03:37.113
Donahue said he walked home.

01:03:39.115 --> 01:03:41.818
Although his route would have taken him past the victim's car,

01:03:42.385 --> 01:03:44.220
Donahue told police he didn't see it.

01:03:45.154 --> 01:03:47.990
He said the holster in his own car belonged to a friend,

01:03:48.391 --> 01:03:49.492
but he didn't have the gun.

01:03:54.664 --> 01:03:55.731
Police were suspicious.

01:03:56.499 --> 01:03:58.734
They asked Donahue for his jacket and boots,

01:03:59.135 --> 01:04:01.571
which might hold clues that told a different story.

01:04:03.973 --> 01:04:05.641
Donahue willingly surrendered them.

01:04:10.446 --> 01:04:11.180
In the darkroom,

01:04:11.581 --> 01:04:13.983
Miller developed one of his most hopeful leads.

01:04:16.319 --> 01:04:17.053
Up until now,

01:04:17.286 --> 01:04:20.122
he'd been merely documenting the various crime scenes.

01:04:20.957 --> 01:04:21.257
Now,

01:04:21.524 --> 01:04:25.895
he put his darkroom skills to the test to flush out blatant clues.

01:04:27.830 --> 01:04:31.534
Using the ruler he placed by the icy footprints when he snapped the photo,

01:04:32.101 --> 01:04:34.570
Miller created a life-size portrait of the prints.

01:04:36.239 --> 01:04:39.308
A boot print can be almost as telling as a fingerprint,

01:04:39.942 --> 01:04:40.943
if it's clear enough.

01:04:43.746 --> 01:04:45.114
Miller was happy with the photo.

01:04:46.782 --> 01:04:47.884
Armed with this image,

01:04:48.651 --> 01:04:52.421
it became just a matter of matching the prints to the boots that made them.

01:04:56.726 --> 01:05:01.964
Miller sent his photographs to a forensic lab for a detailed comparison with Donahue's boots.

01:05:04.066 --> 01:05:07.103
Lab technicians first examined the size of the boots.

01:05:08.104 --> 01:05:09.071
If the prints are off,

01:05:09.272 --> 01:05:10.606
even by a few millimeters,

01:05:11.073 --> 01:05:12.141
there is no match.

01:05:14.777 --> 01:05:16.946
The tread pattern was examined next.

01:05:17.880 --> 01:05:20.149
Technicians poured over the sole of the boot,

01:05:20.750 --> 01:05:24.020
making sure that every detail matched Miller's photograph.

01:05:25.554 --> 01:05:26.055
So far,

01:05:26.355 --> 01:05:26.889
so good.

01:05:28.224 --> 01:05:30.126
But next came the litmus test,

01:05:30.526 --> 01:05:32.962
matching the boot to the foot that wore it.

01:05:33.930 --> 01:05:37.033
It required a close look at the boot's anatomical wear.

01:05:37.867 --> 01:05:43.739
Anatomical wear is the wear that each of us impart into the bottom of the shoe based on how tall we are,

01:05:43.773 --> 01:05:44.640
how much we weigh,

01:05:45.041 --> 01:05:46.042
our bone makeup,

01:05:46.375 --> 01:05:47.076
how we walk,

01:05:47.143 --> 01:05:49.211
whether we walk on the inside of our feet,

01:05:49.278 --> 01:05:50.413
the outside of our feet,

01:05:50.713 --> 01:05:51.714
more so on the heel,

01:05:51.814 --> 01:05:52.848
more so on the toe.

01:05:55.651 --> 01:05:58.154
Donohue wore a lift in one of his boots.

01:05:59.355 --> 01:06:04.694
It added to the unique anatomical wear and left its signature in the boot prints.

01:06:07.029 --> 01:06:07.396
Last,

01:06:07.697 --> 01:06:11.934
technicians examined the boots and the photographs for accidental wear and tear,

01:06:12.601 --> 01:06:14.537
nicks and cuts from daily use.

01:06:16.105 --> 01:06:17.173
In this particular case,

01:06:17.206 --> 01:06:18.741
there are certain marks,

01:06:19.141 --> 01:06:19.909
certain cuts,

01:06:20.343 --> 01:06:34.090
certain indentations in the tread pattern that the examiner was able to see in the photograph and was able to find also in the shoe that he is able to say that this shoe made this impression to the exclusion of all the other shoes in the whole wide world.

01:06:36.525 --> 01:06:39.895
The footprint evidence placed Donahue at the victim's car.

01:06:40.997 --> 01:06:45.301
Buchenberger credits the photographs with keeping the investigation on track.

01:06:46.335 --> 01:06:49.538
If we didn't have the photographs of that shoe impression,

01:06:50.206 --> 01:06:53.943
then we're mainly just stuck with firearms evidence.

01:06:55.111 --> 01:06:56.278
A person might have a gun,

01:06:56.312 --> 01:06:57.680
but who actually shoots that gun?

01:06:59.248 --> 01:07:00.850
But this person wears his own boots.

01:07:03.719 --> 01:07:06.222
Investigators then examined Donahue's jacket,

01:07:06.822 --> 01:07:08.891
combing it meticulously for fibers,

01:07:09.325 --> 01:07:09.592
hair,

01:07:09.992 --> 01:07:10.393
cloth,

01:07:10.826 --> 01:07:13.195
anything that might link Donahue to the victim.

01:07:15.398 --> 01:07:16.932
They found glass particles.

01:07:18.267 --> 01:07:20.403
What new information could they provide?

01:07:22.838 --> 01:07:27.910
Forensic tests determined the glass from the victim's car matched the glass in the parking lot.

01:07:28.677 --> 01:07:31.647
If the glass from Donahue's jacket matched these samples,

01:07:31.981 --> 01:07:37.520
it would place Donahue at the site where the car window was shattered and where the victim was killed.

01:07:38.888 --> 01:07:40.156
The glass matched.

01:07:40.956 --> 01:07:47.129
Police could now prove that Donahue was in the parking lot and maybe in the car at the time of the murder.

01:07:49.965 --> 01:07:52.301
The bloody tire tracks were the next link.

01:07:53.969 --> 01:07:57.073
Because the victim couldn't have driven through her own blood,

01:07:57.606 --> 01:08:03.612
investigators were able to show she was killed in the parking lot and that somebody else moved her car.

01:08:04.313 --> 01:08:04.947
As the car,

01:08:05.514 --> 01:08:05.948
her car,

01:08:06.015 --> 01:08:06.982
was leaving the scene,

01:08:07.416 --> 01:08:10.319
it drove through a pool of blood that was coming from her.

01:08:10.853 --> 01:08:12.588
The blood adhered to the tire,

01:08:12.922 --> 01:08:18.561
and the rotation of the tire through the parking lot kept leaving a blood transfer onto the ground.

01:08:18.694 --> 01:08:19.695
So that's what that was from.

01:08:21.697 --> 01:08:23.833
the scattered clues were coming together,

01:08:24.500 --> 01:08:28.304
thanks in large part to Miller's accurate documentation of the scene.

01:08:31.707 --> 01:08:34.009
The only thing missing was the murder weapon.

01:08:36.112 --> 01:08:37.146
To help pinpoint it,

01:08:37.446 --> 01:08:41.951
investigators tested the bullets and casings found near the victim and her car.

01:08:47.389 --> 01:08:49.925
Another casing was found in Donahue's car.

01:08:52.795 --> 01:08:57.266
Lab tests showed all the casings were fired from a rare Hungarian pistol,

01:08:57.733 --> 01:08:57.867
a

01:08:58.234 --> 01:08:59.869
9mm autoloading Fromer,

01:09:00.369 --> 01:09:03.506
a gun that peaked in popularity around World War II.

01:09:05.641 --> 01:09:11.080
Tests of the impression on the holster from Donahue's car matched the impression a Fromer would make.

01:09:12.348 --> 01:09:14.550
But the gun was nowhere to be found.

01:09:15.584 --> 01:09:17.920
Police scoured the area and came up empty.

01:09:19.188 --> 01:09:19.655
Donahue,

01:09:19.922 --> 01:09:21.724
for all his earlier cooperation,

01:09:22.158 --> 01:09:23.993
drew the line at providing the gun,

01:09:24.760 --> 01:09:26.061
swearing he never had it.

01:09:26.729 --> 01:09:30.332
And he probably thought that whatever he did with this gun,

01:09:30.799 --> 01:09:31.433
and I don't know,

01:09:31.534 --> 01:09:32.801
we never did find that gun,

01:09:34.904 --> 01:09:37.273
probably thought that he was past all this.

01:09:38.541 --> 01:09:39.642
That if we didn't have a gun,

01:09:39.708 --> 01:09:46.182
we probably could not prove that he was the one that actually fired the shot or did anything else.

01:09:47.783 --> 01:09:50.519
But if there ever was a smoking gun in this case,

01:09:50.853 --> 01:09:53.189
it was Miller's photographs of the boot prints,

01:09:53.789 --> 01:09:55.591
something Donahue didn't count on.

01:10:01.096 --> 01:10:04.900
Officer Miller and Detective Guckenberger began to build their case.

01:10:07.670 --> 01:10:08.704
With the glass fragments,

01:10:08.971 --> 01:10:09.905
the shell casings,

01:10:10.172 --> 01:10:10.706
the bullets,

01:10:11.006 --> 01:10:12.908
and the ever-important boot prints,

01:10:13.742 --> 01:10:19.682
they were confident they could prove beyond a reasonable doubt that Gary Asa Donahue was a killer.

01:10:21.116 --> 01:10:21.250
OK,

01:10:21.350 --> 01:10:21.550
great.

01:10:26.322 --> 01:10:29.758
Miller's photographs became primary evidence before a grand jury,

01:10:30.226 --> 01:10:32.127
which sent back Donahue's indictment.

01:10:33.629 --> 01:10:39.702
The photographic clues that Miller provided helped piece together the story of that ill-fated night.

01:10:40.803 --> 01:10:40.970
Okay,

01:10:41.036 --> 01:10:41.203
what

01:10:41.904 --> 01:10:43.439
I believe happened is that

01:10:44.006 --> 01:10:45.808
Mr. Donahue's driving down the street.

01:10:46.342 --> 01:10:48.644
He turns down the wrong street to go to his house,

01:10:48.711 --> 01:10:49.912
probably because he's drunk.

01:10:51.313 --> 01:10:54.750
Donahue lost control of his car and slid off the road.

01:10:55.517 --> 01:10:55.818
Angry,

01:10:56.185 --> 01:10:58.520
he got out of the car and began to walk home.

01:11:00.623 --> 01:11:02.258
As the victim came down the street,

01:11:02.558 --> 01:11:03.826
she recognized the car.

01:11:05.661 --> 01:11:08.097
She likely had seen it in her neighborhood for years.

01:11:10.466 --> 01:11:12.234
Donahue bought it from another neighbor.

01:11:13.736 --> 01:11:15.771
Perhaps she's acting as a good Samaritan.

01:11:16.171 --> 01:11:18.073
She stops to offer him a ride.

01:11:18.374 --> 01:11:20.843
Or perhaps he stepped down on the road and flagged her down.

01:11:21.710 --> 01:11:25.881
The other possibility is he stepped down on the road with a gun and made her stop for him.

01:11:28.017 --> 01:11:30.286
They go down the street to the church parking lot.

01:11:31.453 --> 01:11:34.156
Something occurs inside the car in the church.

01:11:35.057 --> 01:11:36.158
I don't know what that is.

01:11:36.859 --> 01:11:38.260
In homicide investigation,

01:11:38.527 --> 01:11:42.831
you're not always going to be able to answer every single question that you have.

01:11:58.914 --> 01:12:00.215
She falls down to the ground,

01:12:00.683 --> 01:12:02.318
lays down on her back,

01:12:02.484 --> 01:12:02.985
face up.

01:12:03.552 --> 01:12:05.621
Donahue then has a better target,

01:12:05.721 --> 01:12:06.855
a non-moving target.

01:12:07.256 --> 01:12:08.857
He then advances towards her,

01:12:09.291 --> 01:12:10.626
and as she's lying there on the ground,

01:12:10.859 --> 01:12:14.563
he puts the gun right up to her face almost and executes her.

01:12:22.037 --> 01:12:22.905
He leaves the scene,

01:12:23.305 --> 01:12:24.940
drives down to the end of the street,

01:12:25.140 --> 01:12:25.674
where again,

01:12:26.041 --> 01:12:26.842
for whatever reason,

01:12:26.875 --> 01:12:28.077
he runs off the road,

01:12:28.277 --> 01:12:30.079
probably due to his intoxication,

01:12:30.713 --> 01:12:31.580
runs off the road,

01:12:32.381 --> 01:12:33.649
wrecks it into the trees.

01:12:33.982 --> 01:12:34.750
For whatever reason,

01:12:34.783 --> 01:12:36.352
he decides to clear his weapon.

01:12:37.753 --> 01:12:38.787
He works the action,

01:12:39.188 --> 01:12:41.256
the one cartridge casing that's still in the gun,

01:12:41.724 --> 01:12:44.093
ejects out the broken driver's window,

01:12:44.426 --> 01:12:47.730
and is found on the ground next to the vehicle.

01:12:49.665 --> 01:12:50.532
For whatever reason,

01:12:50.833 --> 01:12:54.203
he slid across the seat and left by the passenger's side,

01:12:56.004 --> 01:12:57.840
leaving his boot prints in the snow.

01:13:10.519 --> 01:13:12.087
This is your worst type of killer.

01:13:12.087 --> 01:13:16.325
Because this is the person that kills out of meanness.

01:13:18.260 --> 01:13:20.863
There was no reason at all to kill this woman.

01:13:22.398 --> 01:13:23.298
She was not,

01:13:25.200 --> 01:13:25.467
you know,

01:13:26.201 --> 01:13:30.139
your victim that puts herself in a situation because of a crime.

01:13:30.472 --> 01:13:32.040
She's not that type of person.

01:13:33.642 --> 01:13:37.146
She's the type of person that her innocence is what got her into trouble.

01:13:37.980 --> 01:13:39.715
That day she ran across an animal.

01:13:41.550 --> 01:13:45.053
Gary Asa Donahue was sentenced to life in prison.

01:13:48.657 --> 01:13:54.062
The senseless death of the woman in the church parking lot shocked the residents of her quiet community.

01:13:55.230 --> 01:13:55.998
Further south,

01:13:56.165 --> 01:13:56.665
in Miami,

01:13:56.765 --> 01:13:57.166
Florida,

01:13:57.599 --> 01:13:58.667
murder is more common,

01:13:59.168 --> 01:14:00.502
but no less shocking.

01:14:04.173 --> 01:14:09.378
Through these doors at the Metro Dade County Morgue in Florida come every kind of death,

01:14:09.978 --> 01:14:11.747
both natural and unnatural.

01:14:13.615 --> 01:14:15.250
As bodies come into the morgue,

01:14:15.517 --> 01:14:22.424
it's the job of forensic photographers to document wounds and other identifying traits before the corpses are stored.

01:14:24.159 --> 01:14:27.162
The work is never routine and often tragic.

01:14:28.497 --> 01:14:29.097
On this day,

01:14:29.431 --> 01:14:32.468
a five-year-old girl is the victim of a random shooting.

01:14:33.135 --> 01:14:35.571
She was caught in crossfire during a parade.

01:14:36.772 --> 01:14:39.141
Forensic technicians examine her wounds.

01:14:39.741 --> 01:14:41.777
Perhaps they hold the key to the killer.

01:14:42.077 --> 01:14:43.278
Dade comes to the scene.

01:14:43.412 --> 01:14:47.449
County detectives rely on the information to start their investigation.

01:14:47.749 --> 01:14:49.952
We are trying to determine,

01:14:50.953 --> 01:14:51.587
number one,

01:14:52.421 --> 01:14:57.192
what caliber of round it was that did strike the victim.

01:14:58.193 --> 01:14:59.061
And number two,

01:14:59.261 --> 01:15:04.266
the path of the bullet as it entered the body and then exited.

01:15:05.133 --> 01:15:08.003
That will help us in trying to determine,

01:15:08.036 --> 01:15:08.504
possibly,

01:15:08.537 --> 01:15:09.605
who our shooter is.

01:15:18.847 --> 01:15:20.315
In the medical examiner's office,

01:15:20.582 --> 01:15:24.353
high-tech photography is as much a tool of the trade as a scalpel.

01:15:25.454 --> 01:15:26.889
If a death looks suspicious,

01:15:27.322 --> 01:15:30.459
it's up to forensic photographers to document the details.

01:15:33.829 --> 01:15:39.067
They start with the wound and then photograph individual organs as the autopsy proceeds.

01:15:43.038 --> 01:15:47.109
These gruesome photographs survive long after a case is closed.

01:15:48.377 --> 01:15:53.015
They provide a visual database authorities can refer to if a similar case comes up.

01:15:56.285 --> 01:15:59.521
Knowing what to look for streamlines any investigation.

01:15:59.521 --> 01:16:07.963
In 1986,

01:16:07.963 --> 01:16:10.265
Dade County Police responded to a suicide.

01:16:14.169 --> 01:16:15.771
Jack Sebastian lay dead,

01:16:16.238 --> 01:16:16.371
a

01:16:16.772 --> 01:16:18.407
12-gauge shotgun by his side.

01:16:22.544 --> 01:16:23.612
When death is involved,

01:16:23.845 --> 01:16:26.715
it's standard procedure to capture everything on film.

01:16:27.749 --> 01:16:33.021
The victim's girlfriend told police she was outside when she heard the blast.

01:16:37.659 --> 01:16:39.227
Sebastian left no note.

01:16:43.098 --> 01:16:43.799
At the morgue,

01:16:43.999 --> 01:16:49.972
a staff member felt suspicious about Sebastian's wound and called medical examiner Jay Barnhart.

01:16:50.172 --> 01:16:50.305
Morning,

01:16:50.405 --> 01:16:50.539
Dr.

01:16:50.639 --> 01:16:51.006
Alvarez.

01:16:51.073 --> 01:16:51.573
What do you have?

01:16:53.942 --> 01:16:56.178
Barnhart mapped and measured the wound.

01:16:58.280 --> 01:16:59.414
In his initial opinion,

01:16:59.815 --> 01:17:01.550
this was no suicide.

01:17:03.485 --> 01:17:06.421
Sebastian was shot almost directly between the eyes.

01:17:07.289 --> 01:17:09.291
His wound was surrounded by stippling,

01:17:09.925 --> 01:17:14.596
small speckled burns made by the gunpowder exploding from the shotgun's muzzle.

01:17:16.131 --> 01:17:18.700
If Sebastian held the gun directly to his head,

01:17:19.167 --> 01:17:21.336
the gunpowder wouldn't have marked his face.

01:17:22.471 --> 01:17:23.205
From the stippling,

01:17:23.639 --> 01:17:29.144
Barnhart knew the gun must have been held some distance from Sebastian's forehead when he pulled the trigger.

01:17:30.746 --> 01:17:31.880
Something wasn't right,

01:17:32.347 --> 01:17:34.983
and Barnhart was determined to get to the bottom of it.

01:17:36.418 --> 01:17:36.785
First,

01:17:37.152 --> 01:17:39.988
Barnhart ordered photographs of Sebastian's wounds.

01:17:52.300 --> 01:17:57.472
Then he took x-rays of Sebastian's head to reveal the internal damage he couldn't see.

01:17:59.307 --> 01:18:01.576
The x-ray showed the path of the pellets.

01:18:01.943 --> 01:18:09.651
This is an x-ray showing the dispersion of the birdshot through the skull.

01:18:10.252 --> 01:18:11.920
And if we take the point of entrance,

01:18:11.953 --> 01:18:14.156
which is just above the bridge of the nose...

01:18:14.756 --> 01:18:20.629
and connect that by an imaginary line or series of lines to the location of the pellets,

01:18:21.029 --> 01:18:26.334
we see that the pellets are to the left and below the point of entrance,

01:18:26.668 --> 01:18:31.673
meaning that the shot then was coming from above and to the right of the entrance.

01:18:34.543 --> 01:18:39.881
Barnhart had to find out if Sebastian could hold the gun at the angle indicated by the X-ray.

01:18:40.949 --> 01:18:42.017
It wouldn't have been easy.

01:18:44.186 --> 01:18:50.559
Investigators even placed the shotgun in the extended arms of Sebastian's corpse to see if he could fire the gun.

01:18:51.927 --> 01:18:54.196
His fingers could barely reach the trigger.

01:18:55.897 --> 01:18:57.399
Considering all possibilities,

01:18:57.699 --> 01:19:00.135
investigators tried to fire the gun with their feet.

01:19:01.036 --> 01:19:03.472
They found they could do it with bare toes.

01:19:04.940 --> 01:19:07.242
But Sebastian wore round-toed boots,

01:19:07.676 --> 01:19:10.178
making it impossible for him to fire the gun.

01:19:12.013 --> 01:19:14.116
These extreme tests were necessary.

01:19:15.050 --> 01:19:16.485
Barnhart knew that in court,

01:19:16.818 --> 01:19:19.888
he must be prepared to answer even the strangest questions.

01:19:21.089 --> 01:19:21.857
I ask you things like,

01:19:21.890 --> 01:19:21.990
well,

01:19:21.990 --> 01:19:25.994
couldn't he have been laying in bed and had his foot up over his head and pulled it with his,

01:19:26.595 --> 01:19:28.196
and if he had not been wearing shoes,

01:19:28.563 --> 01:19:29.965
you'd probably have to say yes,

01:19:30.065 --> 01:19:30.499
he could.

01:19:33.668 --> 01:19:36.104
Barnhart wasn't satisfied with his findings.

01:19:36.571 --> 01:19:41.076
He needed to determine the distance between Sebastian and the gun when it fired.

01:19:42.878 --> 01:19:49.751
Barnhart and his staff shot a piece of poster board from distances of one to seven feet and examined the damage.

01:19:51.253 --> 01:19:52.254
From one foot away,

01:19:52.621 --> 01:19:54.623
the force of the shot cracked the board.

01:19:55.390 --> 01:19:56.892
The impact was enormous.

01:19:58.527 --> 01:19:59.227
At two feet,

01:19:59.528 --> 01:20:03.231
the impact was less severe as the buckshot began to spread.

01:20:05.734 --> 01:20:06.434
At four feet,

01:20:06.835 --> 01:20:10.472
the hole roughly doubled in size as the pellets fanned out.

01:20:12.607 --> 01:20:14.009
At six feet from the board,

01:20:14.309 --> 01:20:15.844
the buckshot dispersed further,

01:20:16.344 --> 01:20:18.013
leaving clear pellet marks.

01:20:19.948 --> 01:20:25.821
Barnhart concluded the muzzle of the gun had to be at least one and a half feet away from Sebastian's head.

01:20:26.755 --> 01:20:29.891
He was convinced someone else must have pulled the trigger.

01:20:32.794 --> 01:20:32.928
But...

01:20:33.061 --> 01:20:37.132
Barnhart feared his simple test wasn't enough to sway a jury.

01:20:39.568 --> 01:20:42.304
He turned to the Forensic Imaging Bureau for help.

01:20:44.306 --> 01:20:47.642
Todd Reeves runs Dade County's Forensic Imaging Bureau,

01:20:48.143 --> 01:20:49.878
one of the best in the United States.

01:20:50.879 --> 01:20:55.150
The Bureau has more than 3,000 square feet of advanced photographic equipment.

01:20:56.718 --> 01:21:03.425
The well-equipped studios can document huge pieces of evidence while capturing clues invisible to the naked eye.

01:21:07.495 --> 01:21:07.762
Here,

01:21:07.796 --> 01:21:12.500
a technician uses a microscope-equipped camera to examine a cast of a jawbone.

01:21:14.336 --> 01:21:17.539
Her findings will help match a suspect to a bite mark.

01:21:21.343 --> 01:21:25.280
A potentially useful image may come from a less-than-perfect source,

01:21:25.647 --> 01:21:28.850
like a security camera or shot from a moving vehicle.

01:21:30.118 --> 01:21:31.253
The lab can clean up,

01:21:31.586 --> 01:21:32.187
enlarge,

01:21:32.287 --> 01:21:33.488
and enhance photographs.

01:21:34.422 --> 01:21:37.626
Computers reveal unseen details in evidence photos,

01:21:37.959 --> 01:21:39.561
bringing clues into focus.

01:21:43.865 --> 01:21:49.137
A huge computer database of fingerprints can match even the vaguest finger smudge.

01:21:52.407 --> 01:21:57.178
An editing suite helps investigators assemble persuasive courtroom presentations.

01:21:58.213 --> 01:21:59.214
The higher the quality,

01:21:59.781 --> 01:22:02.050
the better delineation you're giving the jury,

01:22:02.083 --> 01:22:04.085
the more factual information.

01:22:04.653 --> 01:22:06.421
And that's what we're providing is fact,

01:22:06.554 --> 01:22:07.222
not fiction.

01:22:07.555 --> 01:22:11.927
And that's the true message behind professional forensic photography.

01:22:15.830 --> 01:22:19.935
Jay Barnhart knew he had a case for the murder of Jack Sebastian.

01:22:22.137 --> 01:22:26.775
But he'd rely on Reeves and his high-tech tools to make it bulletproof.

01:22:34.282 --> 01:22:36.584
Every fatal wound tells a tale.

01:22:37.419 --> 01:22:38.920
In the case of Jack Sebastian,

01:22:39.187 --> 01:22:41.823
the injuries around the wound completed the story.

01:22:44.392 --> 01:22:45.493
When a gun fires,

01:22:45.727 --> 01:22:47.796
it shoots more than projectiles.

01:22:49.331 --> 01:22:51.499
From the muzzle comes a flash of fire,

01:22:51.933 --> 01:22:53.034
a blast of gas,

01:22:53.568 --> 01:22:54.436
a puff of smoke,

01:22:54.936 --> 01:22:56.371
and a shower of sparks.

01:22:58.540 --> 01:22:59.941
Each can leave its mark.

01:23:00.976 --> 01:23:03.745
Place the muzzle of a gun directly against the target,

01:23:04.145 --> 01:23:06.548
and all this debris goes into the wound.

01:23:07.582 --> 01:23:09.050
Pull the muzzle further away,

01:23:09.451 --> 01:23:11.987
and the debris becomes more and more dispersed.

01:23:14.889 --> 01:23:19.294
The forensic photographs of Sebastian's injuries showed the stippling wounds.

01:23:22.397 --> 01:23:24.766
They also revealed bruising under the eyes,

01:23:25.033 --> 01:23:25.734
on the nose,

01:23:25.867 --> 01:23:26.568
and on the cheek.

01:23:27.335 --> 01:23:31.339
Barnhart believed the damage was caused by the open petals of the shot cup,

01:23:31.840 --> 01:23:34.142
a plastic sleeve that holds the buckshot.

01:23:35.944 --> 01:23:40.949
But how far away would the shotgun have to be to allow the shot cup petals to open?

01:23:42.083 --> 01:23:46.955
Barnhart and Reeves believed high-speed photography could provide the definitive answer.

01:23:48.723 --> 01:23:55.463
It allows the doctors and the police that are investigating the case to be able to see things we can't see with the human eye,

01:23:55.897 --> 01:23:57.899
things that are moving as fast as Mach 15.

01:24:02.003 --> 01:24:05.073
High-speed photography can stop a bullet in mid-air.

01:24:07.809 --> 01:24:08.443
More than that,

01:24:08.843 --> 01:24:10.712
it allows investigators to examine,

01:24:11.046 --> 01:24:12.113
frame by frame,

01:24:12.414 --> 01:24:16.751
the impact and damage that bullet produces as it connects with its target.

01:24:21.222 --> 01:24:23.491
Wounds can then be matched with bullets.

01:24:24.559 --> 01:24:28.263
In cases where no weapon is found or a bullet isn't recovered,

01:24:28.730 --> 01:24:33.134
a series of high-speed photos may help investigators make the identification.

01:24:36.938 --> 01:24:44.012
Reeves and Barnhart designed an experiment to determine the distance between Sebastian's head and the muzzle of the gun that killed him.

01:24:46.981 --> 01:24:47.782
I don't know if there's any...

01:24:47.816 --> 01:24:50.285
I think we can have film in the 35.

01:24:50.652 --> 01:24:54.155
Just gonna crank it in there after it's in the vice.

01:24:54.322 --> 01:24:56.057
They mounted a shotgun on a rack,

01:24:56.524 --> 01:24:57.692
held perfectly level.

01:25:02.297 --> 01:25:02.630
Next,

01:25:02.831 --> 01:25:05.667
they positioned two cameras along the line of fire.

01:25:06.701 --> 01:25:09.070
As the shell and debris left the muzzle,

01:25:09.471 --> 01:25:11.272
they would fly past the cameras,

01:25:11.606 --> 01:25:13.007
which would snap their picture.

01:25:13.675 --> 01:25:16.611
It was crucial that the gun not move when fired.

01:25:17.545 --> 01:25:21.416
They loaded the gun with the same type of ammunition that killed Sebastian.

01:25:23.751 --> 01:25:26.087
Fire in the hole.

01:25:26.187 --> 01:25:26.454
Okay.

01:25:29.390 --> 01:25:33.261
Reeves then placed a sensitive microphone near the muzzle of the gun.

01:25:34.329 --> 01:25:37.332
When the microphone picks up the sound of the shotgun blast,

01:25:37.699 --> 01:25:39.033
it trips a flash unit.

01:25:40.401 --> 01:25:47.242
A ruler placed behind the microphone measures how far the shell travels as the shot cup pedals begin to open.

01:25:50.245 --> 01:25:54.115
The high-speed flash fires for one half millionth of a second,

01:25:55.416 --> 01:25:58.253
necessary when photographing at blazing speeds.

01:26:01.122 --> 01:26:02.223
For the clearest image,

01:26:02.557 --> 01:26:06.861
Reeves and Barnhart need the camera to be as close to the flying shell as possible,

01:26:07.428 --> 01:26:09.597
but must determine the best place to put it.

01:26:11.699 --> 01:26:15.136
They first try the test with a large format Polaroid camera.

01:26:16.304 --> 01:26:22.944
The fast developing film allows them to take test photos of the experiment so they can position the second camera correctly.

01:26:26.748 --> 01:26:27.882
For the actual test,

01:26:28.149 --> 01:26:29.083
they use a standard

01:26:29.817 --> 01:26:34.656
35mm camera with highly sensitive film to freeze the shell in its path.

01:26:36.024 --> 01:26:42.197
The investigators are now ready to try their experiment and catch a shell with a flash of light.

01:26:43.898 --> 01:26:44.866
If all goes well,

01:26:45.233 --> 01:26:49.737
they'll produce an irrefutable record of the shell and its components in flight.

01:26:50.538 --> 01:27:00.215
Barnhart can then use the information to verify where the plastic petals on the shot cup began to open into the pattern that damaged Sebastian's face.

01:27:04.018 --> 01:27:05.587
Each time the gun is fired,

01:27:05.920 --> 01:27:08.590
Reeves inches the microphone away from the muzzle.

01:27:09.524 --> 01:27:10.158
As a result,

01:27:10.391 --> 01:27:12.260
the flash will go off a little later.

01:27:13.294 --> 01:27:15.663
The projectile will travel a little farther.

01:27:16.464 --> 01:27:19.200
The camera will catch it as it opens in flight.

01:27:26.507 --> 01:27:26.975
Initially,

01:27:27.308 --> 01:27:30.178
the white-hot plastic shot cup hasn't expanded.

01:27:33.548 --> 01:27:34.682
After 18 inches,

01:27:34.983 --> 01:27:36.484
the petals begin to spread,

01:27:37.051 --> 01:27:39.320
discharging the pellets and falling away.

01:27:42.824 --> 01:27:43.891
At about 30 inches,

01:27:44.158 --> 01:27:45.727
the petals have fully opened.

01:27:47.695 --> 01:27:54.235
Barnhart and Reeves succeed in making a photographic point-by-point map of a shotgun shell's trajectory.

01:27:57.772 --> 01:27:59.774
By comparing the wound with the pictures,

01:28:00.174 --> 01:28:06.447
investigators determined the gun was fired from a distance of 18 to 24 inches from Sebastian's face.

01:28:08.383 --> 01:28:16.891
The results corroborated Barnhart's own experiments and gave him the evidence he needed to prove Sebastian couldn't have possibly shot himself.

01:28:23.598 --> 01:28:26.134
Police then confronted Sebastian's girlfriend,

01:28:26.401 --> 01:28:27.302
Diane Shelton,

01:28:27.669 --> 01:28:28.536
with the evidence.

01:28:31.673 --> 01:28:37.011
She confessed to pulling the trigger and was sentenced to 12 years in prison.

01:28:39.781 --> 01:28:41.115
Unlike any other tool,

01:28:41.582 --> 01:28:45.720
forensic photography can expose evidence that would otherwise have been missed.

01:28:46.788 --> 01:28:47.588
And sometimes,

01:28:47.922 --> 01:28:52.026
photographs are the only way to prove a crime was committed at all.

01:28:53.328 --> 01:28:58.433
When a Pennsylvania coroner was certain a tragic accident camouflaged a mass murder,

01:28:59.133 --> 01:29:02.003
his photographs became his only allies.

01:29:05.807 --> 01:29:11.713
Forensic photography played a crucial role in the investigation of a devastating fire in a nursing home.

01:29:12.814 --> 01:29:14.082
Retired Wayne County,

01:29:14.182 --> 01:29:21.289
Pennsylvania coroner Robert Jennings spent six years trying to prove the fiery deaths were no accident.

01:29:22.423 --> 01:29:27.595
Jennings has spent much of his life combining his role as coroner and his love of photography.

01:29:28.629 --> 01:29:31.833
He has been snapping shots for more than 45 years.

01:29:35.436 --> 01:29:36.938
We got some fire to the left here.

01:29:36.938 --> 01:29:39.374
Let's go ahead and hide that down before we go in.

01:29:39.374 --> 01:29:42.510
His story begins on the grim night of October 19,

01:29:42.510 --> 01:29:44.212
1971.

01:29:45.413 --> 01:29:49.250
A fire killed all 15 residents of the Geiger Nursing Home,

01:29:49.817 --> 01:29:53.121
a private home attached to a farmhouse near Honesdale,

01:29:53.221 --> 01:29:53.921
Pennsylvania.

01:29:55.356 --> 01:29:57.525
Most of the victims were found in their beds,

01:29:58.025 --> 01:29:59.427
dead from smoke inhalation.

01:30:01.028 --> 01:30:03.731
A few were overcome as they crawled on the floor.

01:30:08.269 --> 01:30:08.903
As coroner,

01:30:09.237 --> 01:30:10.705
Jennings rushed to the scene.

01:30:12.006 --> 01:30:17.912
State police and firefighters had begun removing bodies before Jennings had a chance to document the area.

01:30:19.213 --> 01:30:20.848
After asking the firemen to stop,

01:30:21.416 --> 01:30:23.751
Jennings began photographing all he could.

01:30:24.619 --> 01:30:27.388
The location of the victims could prove important.

01:30:29.123 --> 01:30:34.195
Jennings tried to track the blaze with his camera lens inside the pitch-dark building.

01:30:36.464 --> 01:30:37.832
Time was of the essence.

01:30:40.635 --> 01:30:41.636
He went right to work,

01:30:42.203 --> 01:30:45.339
hoping his pictures could help determine what caused the blaze.

01:30:48.075 --> 01:30:50.912
But the darkness and smoke foiled his efforts.

01:30:59.921 --> 01:31:00.354
Reluctant,

01:31:00.822 --> 01:31:03.090
he went home to await the sunrise.

01:31:06.127 --> 01:31:08.062
By the time he returned the next morning,

01:31:08.463 --> 01:31:13.501
the smoke had cleared and the gloom had lifted enough for him to get his shots of the ruins.

01:31:15.236 --> 01:31:16.704
If your normal F-stop would be

01:31:17.705 --> 01:31:28.015
F11, then you would have to open up at least two more stops and give it more light to get the detail and so forth that's needed in this type of evidence photography,

01:31:28.449 --> 01:31:34.555
that at least arson experts have an opportunity to examine the charring and so forth.

01:31:36.491 --> 01:31:40.862
Working in the crumbling shell of the building was time consuming and treacherous.

01:31:41.729 --> 01:31:45.733
But Jennings was determined to commit every inch of the wreckage to film.

01:31:48.035 --> 01:31:48.803
He succeeded.

01:31:51.472 --> 01:31:54.942
Painting with light revealed details of an alligator burn.

01:31:56.777 --> 01:32:00.281
A charring pattern similar to the rough scales of the reptile.

01:32:01.382 --> 01:32:04.051
The pattern is created at very high temperatures.

01:32:05.786 --> 01:32:08.189
By illuminating the area from several angles,

01:32:08.656 --> 01:32:11.359
Jennings photographed the changes in the burn pattern.

01:32:13.461 --> 01:32:13.728
Later,

01:32:14.095 --> 01:32:17.298
he would refer to his photographs to trace the fire's path.

01:32:20.902 --> 01:32:25.072
Burn patterns on the walls suggested the blaze tore through the hallway.

01:32:26.507 --> 01:32:29.377
A section of the corridor was almost totally consumed.

01:32:30.344 --> 01:32:31.045
In one spot,

01:32:31.312 --> 01:32:33.281
the fire burned a hole in the floor.

01:32:35.283 --> 01:32:39.120
The destruction at this spot was unlike anywhere else in the building.

01:32:40.288 --> 01:32:41.689
Jennings focused his attention,

01:32:42.223 --> 01:32:42.990
and his camera,

01:32:43.558 --> 01:32:44.225
on the hole.

01:32:45.860 --> 01:32:46.827
He crawled under it,

01:32:47.194 --> 01:32:48.462
photographing its edges.

01:32:50.298 --> 01:32:52.567
Fires usually burn from the bottom up,

01:32:53.167 --> 01:32:54.569
igniting whatever's above them.

01:32:55.603 --> 01:32:56.370
But at this spot,

01:32:56.737 --> 01:32:59.340
the fire looked to have burned from the top down.

01:33:00.341 --> 01:33:00.942
To Jennings,

01:33:01.242 --> 01:33:03.511
that suggested an accelerant was used,

01:33:04.111 --> 01:33:06.847
some flammable liquid that seeped through the floor,

01:33:07.214 --> 01:33:08.583
carrying flames with it.

01:33:12.820 --> 01:33:14.989
Before Jennings completed his investigation,

01:33:15.389 --> 01:33:17.358
state police announced their findings.

01:33:18.259 --> 01:33:20.895
They concluded the fire began in the laundry room,

01:33:21.429 --> 01:33:24.365
caused by a bad thermocoupling in a clothes dryer.

01:33:27.101 --> 01:33:28.536
Jennings wasn't buying it.

01:33:29.737 --> 01:33:33.774
He'd spend the next six years trying to prove them wrong.

01:33:35.610 --> 01:33:36.978
Photographs were Jennings'

01:33:37.111 --> 01:33:37.979
only clues.

01:33:38.746 --> 01:33:46.520
The ceiling above the dryer where the state police said the fire started showed minimal damage compared to the highly charred hallway.

01:33:48.656 --> 01:33:51.158
The wires in the laundry room were still intact.

01:33:52.126 --> 01:33:55.463
Jennings even found unburned clothing inside the dryer.

01:33:57.898 --> 01:33:58.899
Picture by picture,

01:33:59.100 --> 01:34:01.002
he continued building his case.

01:34:22.256 --> 01:34:22.623
Today,

01:34:22.990 --> 01:34:24.358
a quarter century later...

01:34:24.792 --> 01:34:25.226
Jennings'

01:34:25.326 --> 01:34:26.360
evidence remains,

01:34:26.894 --> 01:34:28.796
though the building is long gone.

01:34:30.531 --> 01:34:32.199
Within a few weeks after the fire,

01:34:32.566 --> 01:34:35.169
the building is totally destroyed and demolished,

01:34:35.469 --> 01:34:35.936
razed.

01:34:36.537 --> 01:34:37.204
So therefore,

01:34:38.205 --> 01:34:39.440
the evidence that I photographed,

01:34:39.473 --> 01:34:40.107
that's gone.

01:34:40.841 --> 01:34:42.910
And if you don't have the photographs there to support,

01:34:43.110 --> 01:34:43.911
to look it over,

01:34:45.312 --> 01:34:46.714
it's pretty hard to solve a crime.

01:34:51.252 --> 01:34:51.886
For Jennings,

01:34:52.219 --> 01:34:57.358
not much has changed in the art and science of photography since he snapped his first picture.

01:34:58.426 --> 01:34:58.793
Today,

01:34:59.160 --> 01:35:02.530
as he did 25 years ago in the Geiger nursing home case,

01:35:02.997 --> 01:35:05.433
he processes his own photographs by hand,

01:35:06.067 --> 01:35:08.602
carefully controlling every step of exposure,

01:35:09.070 --> 01:35:09.637
developing,

01:35:09.937 --> 01:35:10.538
and printing.

01:35:13.841 --> 01:35:15.209
He's sharpened his skills,

01:35:15.810 --> 01:35:18.179
both as a photographer and an investigator.

01:35:19.947 --> 01:35:24.051
He's gained a better sense of what to look for and how to capture it on film.

01:35:26.120 --> 01:35:27.588
A charred piece of timber,

01:35:28.055 --> 01:35:29.123
a pile of debris.

01:35:30.357 --> 01:35:35.029
These seemingly trivial objects can be clues that make or break a case.

01:35:37.331 --> 01:35:40.634
Jennings has to be sure no detail is overlooked.

01:35:42.636 --> 01:35:46.006
An investigation is a lot like a roll of exposed film.

01:35:47.141 --> 01:35:48.476
All the information is there.

01:35:49.076 --> 01:35:51.445
You just have to know how to make it visible.

01:35:53.514 --> 01:35:54.315
In his darkroom,

01:35:54.715 --> 01:35:56.717
Jennings exposes many secrets.

01:35:57.485 --> 01:35:58.319
And in the process,

01:35:58.586 --> 01:36:01.188
he sometimes develops even more questions.

01:36:05.593 --> 01:36:07.628
State police were impressed by Jennings'

01:36:07.628 --> 01:36:08.262
photographs,

01:36:08.662 --> 01:36:11.198
but still wouldn't concede the fire was arson.

01:36:14.401 --> 01:36:16.771
They didn't waiver for one simple reason.

01:36:18.372 --> 01:36:19.240
No suspect.

01:36:21.108 --> 01:36:23.010
If the fire had been deliberately set,

01:36:23.511 --> 01:36:24.512
who lit the match?

01:36:25.579 --> 01:36:26.113
In the end,

01:36:26.547 --> 01:36:26.981
Jennings'

01:36:27.047 --> 01:36:28.449
pictures exposed him.

01:36:30.551 --> 01:36:33.087
Hoping to prove the fire was deliberately set,

01:36:33.687 --> 01:36:35.990
Jennings turned to Nurse Vaudine Lyon,

01:36:36.457 --> 01:36:38.225
the sole survivor of the blaze.

01:36:39.093 --> 01:36:41.896
She told him about one of the home's gentlest patients,

01:36:42.296 --> 01:36:42.530
Mr.

01:36:42.596 --> 01:36:43.430
Maurice Flynn.

01:36:45.032 --> 01:36:45.299
Mr.

01:36:45.366 --> 01:36:45.699
Flynn,

01:36:46.367 --> 01:36:48.435
a deeply shy and private man,

01:36:48.836 --> 01:36:50.371
would end each day in his room,

01:36:50.871 --> 01:36:52.573
reading the Bible by candlelight.

01:36:55.676 --> 01:36:57.745
So it was odd that after the fire,

01:36:58.012 --> 01:37:01.215
Flynn's body was found seated in the room of a married couple.

01:37:02.249 --> 01:37:06.020
He was the only patient not in his own room when the fire occurred.

01:37:07.021 --> 01:37:10.090
He was also the only patient found completely naked.

01:37:11.158 --> 01:37:12.293
Scarcely burned,

01:37:12.626 --> 01:37:18.365
he died seated in a chair with his arms and legs extended as though crucified.

01:37:18.365 --> 01:37:20.601
He was known as the gentleman of the house.

01:37:21.001 --> 01:37:22.169
He was there to help people.

01:37:23.737 --> 01:37:24.939
But he was very private.

01:37:25.139 --> 01:37:29.176
He wouldn't even leave a nurse or anyone with him when he's taking a shower.

01:37:29.476 --> 01:37:30.744
He just didn't want any help.

01:37:31.378 --> 01:37:37.284
And this was always a puzzling part as to why this particular person was found this distance away.

01:37:39.086 --> 01:37:43.090
Flynn's own room was some 60 feet from where his body was found.

01:37:43.724 --> 01:37:47.361
The smoke and flame would have made that simple walk impossible.

01:37:49.230 --> 01:37:53.100
Jennings determined the blaze started at the intersection of the hallways.

01:37:54.034 --> 01:37:57.137
In order for Flynn to get to the couple's room during the fire,

01:37:57.538 --> 01:37:59.440
he would have had to pass right through it.

01:38:01.342 --> 01:38:02.142
On the other hand,

01:38:02.476 --> 01:38:04.144
if he wanted to escape the blaze,

01:38:04.378 --> 01:38:06.046
the exit was near his room,

01:38:06.547 --> 01:38:07.882
easily within his reach.

01:38:09.083 --> 01:38:17.791
And it's impossible that he could have come down that distance and through that T-intersection with the type of physical evidence that we found on his body,

01:38:18.492 --> 01:38:22.029
because he would have never made it and he would have been completely charred.

01:38:26.033 --> 01:38:33.407
Jennings wondered why Flynn was so far from his room while most everyone else died in bed from the fast-spreading blaze.

01:38:36.043 --> 01:38:36.710
On June 8,

01:38:36.710 --> 01:38:38.178
1972,

01:38:38.679 --> 01:38:41.548
Robert Jennings exhumed the body of Maurice Flynn.

01:38:43.484 --> 01:38:44.785
Even before the autopsy,

01:38:45.152 --> 01:38:46.720
Jennings noticed something odd.

01:38:47.755 --> 01:38:49.957
Flynn's skin was almost cherry red,

01:38:50.591 --> 01:38:52.726
indicating carbon monoxide poisoning.

01:38:53.694 --> 01:38:59.500
The autopsy showed Flynn's lung tissue had absorbed gasoline vapors shortly before he died.

01:39:02.069 --> 01:39:04.538
More clues came out during Lyon's interview.

01:39:06.273 --> 01:39:09.877
Flynn was the only resident in the home allowed to have matches.

01:39:10.678 --> 01:39:14.081
Matches to light the candles by which he read his Bible.

01:39:15.182 --> 01:39:17.851
He also mowed the lawn around the nursing home,

01:39:18.285 --> 01:39:22.423
making him one of the only residents with access to gasoline.

01:39:33.067 --> 01:39:36.036
Flynn behaved strangely the day before the fire,

01:39:36.670 --> 01:39:38.772
ranting and citing biblical verse.

01:39:46.680 --> 01:39:49.249
The home's other residents also acted strangely.

01:39:50.250 --> 01:39:54.455
They paid off forgotten debts and talked of a journey to a better place.

01:39:55.022 --> 01:39:59.960
Everything centered around the strange events of Mr.

01:40:00.060 --> 01:40:00.327
Flynn,

01:40:01.362 --> 01:40:03.697
why he was completely naked.

01:40:04.131 --> 01:40:07.868
One of his comments to one of the nurses on duty that day was that you come in this...

01:40:09.036 --> 01:40:11.005
a world naked and you leave it naked.

01:40:11.672 --> 01:40:16.043
And that stuck in our minds a lot with finding his completely nude body.

01:40:19.713 --> 01:40:22.082
It looked like Jennings had his suspect.

01:40:24.418 --> 01:40:27.421
But state police wouldn't revise their conclusions.

01:40:28.355 --> 01:40:29.556
Jennings persisted.

01:40:31.058 --> 01:40:33.027
By early 1975,

01:40:33.360 --> 01:40:36.864
Jennings was confident that he had enough evidence to prove his case.

01:40:37.865 --> 01:40:42.736
He exercised his powers as coroner and swore in a jury of six men and women.

01:40:43.771 --> 01:40:49.510
He presented his findings while the state police defended their position during the two-day legal battle.

01:40:51.111 --> 01:40:51.578
Afterward,

01:40:51.845 --> 01:40:57.684
the jury concluded the fire was caused by an illegal act by a person or persons unknown.

01:41:01.488 --> 01:41:06.527
They ruled that investigators should attempt to discover who caused the 15 deaths.

01:41:08.729 --> 01:41:11.732
Why Flynn set the fire will never be known.

01:41:12.900 --> 01:41:16.870
Some have speculated that his actions were the result of a deranged mind.

01:41:19.073 --> 01:41:23.577
Others have said Flynn and the other nursing home residents have all agreed to die,

01:41:24.111 --> 01:41:27.481
thinking it better to end their lives than live in the home.

01:41:30.717 --> 01:41:31.285
In the end,

01:41:31.685 --> 01:41:37.291
the closest we may ever come to finding out the cause of the fire lies in Jennings'

01:41:37.324 --> 01:41:40.527
photographs and his efforts at solving this crime.

01:41:42.029 --> 01:41:43.897
After the coroner jury's verdict,

01:41:44.331 --> 01:41:48.535
Jennings filled out the death certificates of the residents of the Geiger nursing home,

01:41:50.771 --> 01:41:52.106
14 homicides,

01:41:54.308 --> 01:41:55.242
and a suicide.

01:41:56.276 --> 01:41:57.845
It was worth the effort.

01:41:58.445 --> 01:42:06.453
I felt that I had a duty to represent the other 14 people that paid a price their lives in this particular incident,

01:42:06.453 --> 01:42:09.189
and particularly for the families as well.

01:42:12.659 --> 01:42:16.263
Forensic photography is the new detective's secret weapon.

01:42:17.464 --> 01:42:18.332
Often overlooked,

01:42:18.732 --> 01:42:21.135
it is crucial in a criminal investigation.

01:42:22.536 --> 01:42:24.271
With sophisticated camera equipment,

01:42:24.838 --> 01:42:26.940
determination and ingenuity,

01:42:27.841 --> 01:42:32.513
forensic photographers become the eyes and memories of investigators.

01:42:33.647 --> 01:42:36.350
They are also the advocates of the victims,

01:42:37.084 --> 01:42:39.486
helping bring criminals to justice.

01:42:44.558 --> 01:42:47.594
Investigators in Canada are on the trail of a killer.

01:42:49.596 --> 01:42:51.899
The key lies in the victim's cells.

01:42:54.368 --> 01:42:56.703
But has fire destroyed all the evidence?

01:43:00.641 --> 01:43:01.542
At a murder scene,

01:43:01.775 --> 01:43:04.511
a handful of cigarettes provides the only clue.

01:43:06.747 --> 01:43:09.883
Will forensic detectives be able to unlock their secret?

01:43:12.019 --> 01:43:15.689
This young man was imprisoned for a rape he swears he didn't commit.

01:43:16.723 --> 01:43:18.659
Can science prove he's innocent?

01:43:19.993 --> 01:43:21.128
When police are stumped,

01:43:21.495 --> 01:43:27.267
they may turn to detectives in lab coats to uncover clues hidden inside the human cell.

01:43:28.569 --> 01:43:33.707
One strand of DNA can finger a killer or set the innocent free.

01:44:04.037 --> 01:44:06.440
In July 1991,

01:44:06.440 --> 01:44:07.307
a janitor in Vancouver,

01:44:07.341 --> 01:44:09.910
British Columbia made a routine trip to the dumpster.

01:44:11.311 --> 01:44:13.947
But what he found was far from routine.

01:44:15.415 --> 01:44:18.018
Inside lay a body burned so completely,

01:44:18.352 --> 01:44:19.920
he wasn't sure it was human.

01:44:21.021 --> 01:44:22.522
He ran to call police.

01:44:28.528 --> 01:44:29.162
Across town,

01:44:29.496 --> 01:44:33.000
detectives Al Catley and Rick Crook responded to the call.

01:44:37.404 --> 01:44:40.307
The homicide investigators had done this drill before.

01:44:42.576 --> 01:44:46.947
What may look like a human body sometimes turns out to be a practical joke,

01:44:47.914 --> 01:44:49.016
a discarded mannequin,

01:44:49.516 --> 01:44:51.451
or some other case of mistaken identity.

01:44:53.487 --> 01:44:55.522
They hoped that was all it was.

01:44:57.024 --> 01:44:57.291
Still,

01:44:57.691 --> 01:44:58.825
they had to check it out.

01:45:00.894 --> 01:45:03.463
So when we walked over to take a look inside the dumpster,

01:45:04.464 --> 01:45:07.334
there was just the outline of a human body.

01:45:08.335 --> 01:45:08.835
And in fact,

01:45:08.835 --> 01:45:20.881
it was so badly destroyed by the fire that it was questionable as to whether it was human or we've had some instances of bear carcasses being destroyed after illegal hunting or something like that.

01:45:21.014 --> 01:45:22.549
And we didn't rule out that possibility.

01:45:26.053 --> 01:45:28.155
The officers approached the dumpster carefully,

01:45:28.822 --> 01:45:31.625
making note of details that could become important later.

01:45:36.330 --> 01:45:39.066
The dumpster was cool to the touch when they arrived.

01:45:39.833 --> 01:45:41.635
The fire had been out for hours.

01:45:46.139 --> 01:45:48.975
A stain spread from the corner onto the pavement.

01:45:49.976 --> 01:45:54.815
Whoever set the blaze used more than enough flammable liquid to destroy the contents.

01:45:56.850 --> 01:46:01.054
A close inspection of the contents confirmed that this was no false alarm.

01:46:02.255 --> 01:46:02.556
There,

01:46:02.956 --> 01:46:04.958
amid the ashes of computer boxes,

01:46:05.225 --> 01:46:05.992
wooden flats,

01:46:06.026 --> 01:46:06.793
and scrap paper,

01:46:07.461 --> 01:46:08.528
lay a human body.

01:46:11.765 --> 01:46:13.533
After the detectives made their notes,

01:46:13.867 --> 01:46:16.770
the delicate removal of the fragile evidence began.

01:46:17.904 --> 01:46:22.042
It was gently placed in a body bag so as not to damage it any further.

01:46:23.477 --> 01:46:24.745
Then it went to the morgue,

01:46:25.078 --> 01:46:28.382
where a pathologist would examine the body for clues to its identity.

01:46:29.583 --> 01:46:33.420
Anything else that survived the blaze was also collected and sealed.

01:46:34.354 --> 01:46:35.122
Not surprising.

01:46:35.122 --> 01:46:36.223
Surveying the scene,

01:46:36.223 --> 01:46:39.626
the detectives realized the murderer selected his site carefully,

01:46:40.527 --> 01:46:42.996
an industrial park closed for the weekend,

01:46:43.530 --> 01:46:45.465
surrounded by brush and trees.

01:46:46.099 --> 01:46:46.400
In fact,

01:46:46.400 --> 01:46:49.002
we did establish that people were driving by.

01:46:49.436 --> 01:46:53.140
Roughly the time that we think that the homicide occurred,

01:46:53.173 --> 01:46:54.541
or the burning occurred,

01:46:55.175 --> 01:46:56.276
and there wasn't anything.

01:46:56.676 --> 01:47:02.983
You could have quite easily have driven up here and probably out without really seeing what you would have thought was a fire.

01:47:04.584 --> 01:47:07.721
Homicides are often solved in the first 24 hours,

01:47:07.988 --> 01:47:10.157
when investigators follow hot leads.

01:47:10.991 --> 01:47:11.725
But in this case,

01:47:11.992 --> 01:47:13.226
the fire had gone out.

01:47:13.760 --> 01:47:15.228
The trail had grown cold.

01:47:18.565 --> 01:47:20.967
If the murder happened just five years earlier,

01:47:21.501 --> 01:47:22.536
Crook and Catley would have been

01:47:22.536 --> 01:47:28.041
have relied solely on hunches and their talent for bulldogging a case until they had the answers.

01:47:30.844 --> 01:47:32.212
But by 1991,

01:47:32.546 --> 01:47:33.680
when this victim died,

01:47:34.114 --> 01:47:37.150
Crook and Catley knew they had an ally in the lab.

01:47:38.618 --> 01:47:41.588
A powerful new tool had been added to crime solving.

01:47:42.722 --> 01:47:44.024
DNA profiling.

01:47:46.927 --> 01:47:50.597
Investigators no longer needed a shred of evidence to solve a case.

01:47:51.364 --> 01:47:53.366
They only needed a single cell.

01:47:56.036 --> 01:47:58.972
As personal as the whorls and lines of a fingerprint,

01:47:59.473 --> 01:48:01.241
but far more difficult to hide,

01:48:01.975 --> 01:48:05.245
DNA is the next wave in scientific sleuthing.

01:48:07.247 --> 01:48:07.547
Today,

01:48:08.048 --> 01:48:12.819
scientists can extract DNA on a sweat-stained hat left by a nervous killer,

01:48:13.987 --> 01:48:16.890
from saliva on the stamp of an extortionist's letter,

01:48:18.091 --> 01:48:22.762
or from dried blood or skin scraped from underneath a victim's fingernails.

01:48:27.934 --> 01:48:31.705
Microscopic evidence has always been left behind by criminals.

01:48:32.706 --> 01:48:36.977
Only with the advent of DNA profiling has it become incriminating.

01:48:40.280 --> 01:48:40.747
DNA,

01:48:41.081 --> 01:48:43.183
or deoxyribonucleic acid,

01:48:43.517 --> 01:48:45.986
contains every detail that makes you human.

01:48:47.787 --> 01:48:48.722
No two people,

01:48:49.055 --> 01:48:50.357
except identical twins,

01:48:50.824 --> 01:48:52.225
share the same patterns.

01:48:56.429 --> 01:49:00.100
DNA is small enough to fit inside the nucleus of every cell.

01:49:01.134 --> 01:49:02.836
Its shape is a double helix,

01:49:03.436 --> 01:49:06.239
like a spiral staircase wrapped around a column.

01:49:07.774 --> 01:49:08.408
Uncoiled,

01:49:08.742 --> 01:49:10.911
the double helix would stretch to eight feet.

01:49:13.246 --> 01:49:15.181
About 99.5%

01:49:15.248 --> 01:49:17.317
of everyone's DNA is the same,

01:49:18.218 --> 01:49:21.288
but some sections vary greatly from person to person.

01:49:22.222 --> 01:49:23.690
By studying those sections,

01:49:23.990 --> 01:49:27.694
scientists can identify victims and unmask murderers.

01:49:30.630 --> 01:49:33.366
In the Virginia Division of Forensic Science in Richmond,

01:49:33.833 --> 01:49:38.204
Paul Ferrara runs one of the most progressive DNA testing labs in the country.

01:49:38.905 --> 01:49:41.041
With the sensitivity of this technology,

01:49:41.474 --> 01:49:50.517
I don't know that I could go in and commit a violent crime and just not somehow leave something of me behind.

01:49:51.451 --> 01:49:53.486
And that's all it takes with this technology,

01:49:53.620 --> 01:49:55.188
just some little piece.

01:49:55.956 --> 01:49:59.726
of me that's got a cell with a nucleus in it,

01:50:00.393 --> 01:50:01.595
and I'm in deep trouble.

01:50:05.632 --> 01:50:07.400
Heat is the enemy of DNA,

01:50:08.101 --> 01:50:14.174
and fire is the bane of forensic investigators who try to recover DNA from torched crime scenes.

01:50:15.642 --> 01:50:17.377
A week after the victim was found,

01:50:17.844 --> 01:50:23.016
Crook and firefighters recreated the inferno to see if it could shed some light on the crime.

01:50:27.687 --> 01:50:29.422
Whether by design or accident,

01:50:29.756 --> 01:50:31.391
the killer left one lid open,

01:50:31.891 --> 01:50:34.260
creating a downdraft inside the dumpster.

01:50:35.962 --> 01:50:38.632
The rushing air whipped the flames into a frenzy.

01:50:46.640 --> 01:50:50.110
The surrounding trees hid what little smoke the fire produced.

01:50:53.313 --> 01:50:57.050
It burned at about 2,000 degrees for 30 to 40 minutes.

01:50:58.518 --> 01:50:59.285
By comparison,

01:50:59.586 --> 01:51:05.058
a crematorium burns between 1,000 to 1,400 degrees for about three hours.

01:51:05.992 --> 01:51:08.595
Police were lucky to have any remains at all.

01:51:10.597 --> 01:51:12.966
Now they had to figure out who was killed here.

01:51:14.300 --> 01:51:18.271
All the pathologist could tell them was that the victim was a white female.

01:51:20.774 --> 01:51:22.575
Traditional methods at a standstill.

01:51:23.076 --> 01:51:24.911
Detectives focused on x-rays.

01:51:26.346 --> 01:51:28.381
They showed the victim was shot in the head.

01:51:29.149 --> 01:51:32.619
But they also revealed a feature detectives hoped would identify her.

01:51:35.555 --> 01:51:36.222
David Sweet,

01:51:36.489 --> 01:51:39.426
a forensic odontologist or dental investigator,

01:51:39.693 --> 01:51:40.593
worked on the case.

01:51:42.529 --> 01:51:47.333
Teeth are the hardest substance in the human body and the most difficult to destroy.

01:51:48.635 --> 01:51:50.737
When bodies can't be easily identified,

01:51:51.104 --> 01:51:54.441
odontologists look to the teeth to tell them about the victim.

01:51:55.608 --> 01:51:59.212
Their wear and condition can hold the story of a person's life.

01:52:00.080 --> 01:52:01.681
Whether they were right or left-handed,

01:52:02.282 --> 01:52:04.317
drank water with fluoride as a child,

01:52:04.718 --> 01:52:05.618
or wore braces.

01:52:06.753 --> 01:52:08.188
By studying the victim's teeth,

01:52:08.555 --> 01:52:10.390
it was determined that she was in her early

01:52:10.890 --> 01:52:10.990
20s.

01:52:12.025 --> 01:52:13.927
She had one extraordinary feature.

01:52:14.260 --> 01:52:23.236
But the other thing that we realized when we examined the teeth was there was an extra tooth right in the center of the upper jaw between the upper central incisors.

01:52:23.636 --> 01:52:28.575
And those showed up very well in the x-rays that were taken after the victim died.

01:52:29.976 --> 01:52:34.881
Crook ran a computer search that compared his findings with dental records of missing persons.

01:52:36.316 --> 01:52:37.150
None matched.

01:52:38.151 --> 01:52:39.385
Despite his best efforts,

01:52:39.652 --> 01:52:40.620
Crook was stymied.

01:52:41.254 --> 01:52:45.358
He still had a victim without a name and a murder without a suspect.

01:52:46.793 --> 01:52:47.293
Meanwhile,

01:52:47.660 --> 01:52:48.495
somewhere out there,

01:52:48.561 --> 01:52:49.929
a killer ran free,

01:52:50.597 --> 01:52:53.133
confident nothing could link him to his crime.

01:52:54.567 --> 01:52:59.806
What he didn't know was that a small piece of evidence could provide the missing link.

01:53:06.079 --> 01:53:06.846
For two days,

01:53:07.113 --> 01:53:09.349
Detective Al Catley chased a ghost.

01:53:10.150 --> 01:53:12.018
Who was the woman in the dumpster?

01:53:12.752 --> 01:53:15.388
What awful chain of events led to her murder?

01:53:16.589 --> 01:53:18.258
Surely by now someone,

01:53:18.591 --> 01:53:19.192
a friend,

01:53:19.392 --> 01:53:19.893
a lover,

01:53:20.160 --> 01:53:20.894
a co-worker,

01:53:21.327 --> 01:53:22.061
was missing her.

01:53:23.296 --> 01:53:26.199
Catley asked his staff for missing persons reports.

01:53:27.167 --> 01:53:28.101
One stood out.

01:53:29.035 --> 01:53:29.803
Lynn Breeden,

01:53:30.236 --> 01:53:30.804
age 30.

01:53:31.838 --> 01:53:33.373
Her roommate filed the report.

01:53:34.207 --> 01:53:36.109
He had dropped her off at a friend's house.

01:53:37.043 --> 01:53:39.846
The friend saw her leaving a nightclub around 3 a.m.,

01:53:40.180 --> 01:53:42.615
the night before the burned woman was found.

01:53:43.449 --> 01:53:45.151
She left with an unknown man.

01:53:47.353 --> 01:53:48.154
Three days later,

01:53:48.488 --> 01:53:51.891
a woman posing as Lynn Breeden tried to empty her bank account.

01:53:55.929 --> 01:53:59.365
The woman presented a withdrawal slip with Breeden's account number.

01:54:03.837 --> 01:54:03.970
Hi,

01:54:04.003 --> 01:54:06.773
I need to make a withdrawal.

01:54:06.773 --> 01:54:09.108
The teller knew she wasn't who she claimed to be.

01:54:11.511 --> 01:54:12.846
When she questioned the imposter,

01:54:13.813 --> 01:54:15.081
the woman became flustered.

01:54:18.751 --> 01:54:20.453
When the teller tried to detain the woman,

01:54:20.854 --> 01:54:21.621
she hurried out.

01:54:21.821 --> 01:54:22.856
I need you to stay here.

01:54:23.022 --> 01:54:23.156
Wait,

01:54:23.223 --> 01:54:23.656
I gotta go.

01:54:24.157 --> 01:54:25.391
I need you to stay here.

01:54:31.297 --> 01:54:34.033
A security camera captured the failed transaction.

01:54:35.068 --> 01:54:39.172
Analysis of the film revealed a man waiting for the woman in the parking lot.

01:54:40.640 --> 01:54:44.244
Police made a photo of the woman from the tape and ran it in the newspaper.

01:54:45.278 --> 01:54:47.780
She was identified and called in for questioning.

01:54:49.449 --> 01:54:53.586
She told police a friend had offered her $500 to scam the bank.

01:54:54.387 --> 01:54:55.688
She knew nothing of the murder.

01:54:56.923 --> 01:54:58.558
The friend was Chris Cruz,

01:54:58.958 --> 01:55:02.362
an exotic dancer who used the stage name Tony Devins.

01:55:03.529 --> 01:55:05.832
Detectives placed him under constant surveillance,

01:55:06.366 --> 01:55:08.701
then asked him to come in to speak with police.

01:55:10.336 --> 01:55:12.138
Not realizing police were watching him,

01:55:12.572 --> 01:55:16.743
Cruz parked his car two blocks from the station and walked the rest of the way.

01:55:18.544 --> 01:55:24.183
The surveillance officers called Sergeant Crook to tell him they thought Cruz must be hiding something in his car.

01:55:25.919 --> 01:55:27.520
When Cruz arrived at the station,

01:55:27.921 --> 01:55:29.722
police asked if they could search his car.

01:55:31.090 --> 01:55:32.892
He told them the car was at his parents'

01:55:33.159 --> 01:55:33.960
in another county.

01:55:35.361 --> 01:55:36.529
They knew he was lying.

01:55:37.397 --> 01:55:39.232
They told him they knew where he parked it,

01:55:39.532 --> 01:55:41.434
and they didn't need his permission to search it.

01:55:42.568 --> 01:55:43.102
Reluctantly,

01:55:43.503 --> 01:55:44.938
he handed over the keys.

01:55:51.277 --> 01:55:54.614
Forensic investigators took pictures of the car from every angle,

01:55:55.248 --> 01:55:58.017
carefully recording what they saw before touching it.

01:55:59.285 --> 01:56:01.688
They moved in closer to gather more evidence,

01:56:02.255 --> 01:56:04.691
collecting and photographing virtually everything.

01:56:05.858 --> 01:56:09.095
It was too soon to tell what might be important later.

01:56:12.732 --> 01:56:14.300
The men worked in teams.

01:56:15.201 --> 01:56:16.936
Some officers inspected the evidence,

01:56:17.370 --> 01:56:19.439
while others marked where it was found.

01:56:23.543 --> 01:56:27.313
Investigators found blood stains on the sides and rear of the car.

01:56:28.982 --> 01:56:30.683
They marked the positions carefully.

01:56:32.251 --> 01:56:32.518
Later,

01:56:32.852 --> 01:56:39.025
the shape of the stains would be analyzed to calculate what direction the blood came from and its point of origin.

01:56:40.126 --> 01:56:43.062
These details are crucial for reconstructing a crime.

01:56:45.465 --> 01:56:48.768
Blood samples may also be collected for DNA analysis.

01:56:55.441 --> 01:56:57.610
Police search the car inside and out.

01:57:00.346 --> 01:57:02.448
They look through personal items in the back seat,

01:57:03.049 --> 01:57:06.753
hoping to find any piece of evidence to tie the victim to the car.

01:57:11.257 --> 01:57:11.724
All right,

01:57:11.824 --> 01:57:13.059
it's 10.05.

01:57:13.126 --> 01:57:14.260
We're going into the trunk.

01:57:16.362 --> 01:57:16.662
Next,

01:57:16.963 --> 01:57:17.830
they open the trunk.

01:57:19.232 --> 01:57:19.332
Ooh,

01:57:19.332 --> 01:57:21.200
look at this right off the bat.

01:57:22.168 --> 01:57:22.668
Inside,

01:57:22.935 --> 01:57:26.072
they found a gas can and other objects covered with blood.

01:57:26.139 --> 01:57:27.040
Get a shot.

01:57:27.507 --> 01:57:28.341
One moment.

01:57:28.775 --> 01:57:29.709
Beneath some clothing,

01:57:30.009 --> 01:57:32.211
they found several key pieces of evidence,

01:57:32.879 --> 01:57:37.050
a bloody tire iron and a .22 caliber rifle.

01:57:37.083 --> 01:57:38.017
I'd say that's the deal.

01:57:38.017 --> 01:57:42.221
The detectives now were almost certain Cruz killed Lynn Breeden.

01:57:42.789 --> 01:57:45.758
But they had no way of connecting the suspect to the victim.

01:57:46.859 --> 01:57:48.327
Cruz denied knowing her.

01:57:49.162 --> 01:57:52.565
He told friends he bought her identification on the black market,

01:57:53.433 --> 01:57:56.669
and police couldn't prove the blood on the car was hers.

01:57:58.571 --> 01:58:03.276
They needed to compare the DNA from the blood on the car to DNA from the victim.

01:58:04.610 --> 01:58:07.146
But where could they find the victim's DNA?

01:58:09.015 --> 01:58:10.817
They turned to David Sweet for help.

01:58:12.051 --> 01:58:15.121
He suggested looking for it in her impacted wisdom teeth.

01:58:16.589 --> 01:58:18.391
No one had ever tried it before,

01:58:18.991 --> 01:58:20.460
but it was their last hope.

01:58:20.960 --> 01:58:27.266
We know theoretically that there are cells still embedded in the mineral tissues of the teeth,

01:58:27.600 --> 01:58:29.302
and we should be able to open the teeth,

01:58:29.535 --> 01:58:31.904
extract those cellular materials,

01:58:32.238 --> 01:58:36.909
and extract the DNA from the cells and then process it in a forensic DNA analysis.

01:58:38.711 --> 01:58:42.849
A tooth is an armor coating that surrounds a core of living tissue.

01:58:43.983 --> 01:58:50.456
Sweet hoped the tissue within the impacted wisdom teeth were protected from the fire by the thick jaw bones surrounding them.

01:58:52.592 --> 01:58:55.328
He cut the first wisdom tooth from crown to root,

01:58:56.496 --> 01:58:58.297
but found no tissue inside.

01:59:00.099 --> 01:59:02.001
His second attempt was more successful.

01:59:02.702 --> 01:59:03.603
He found tissue,

01:59:03.936 --> 01:59:07.039
but he didn't know if its DNA was destroyed by the fire.

01:59:08.241 --> 01:59:10.309
He sent it to the lab to be tested.

01:59:11.511 --> 01:59:13.346
All he could do was wait.

01:59:20.953 --> 01:59:23.189
Did Chris Cruz kill Lynn Breeden?

01:59:24.023 --> 01:59:26.626
The answer was in a forensic lab in Vancouver,

01:59:27.059 --> 01:59:29.362
where the victim's tissue was sent for testing.

01:59:30.496 --> 01:59:30.897
Finally,

01:59:31.297 --> 01:59:31.631
Dr.

01:59:31.697 --> 01:59:33.199
Sweet received the results.

01:59:33.733 --> 01:59:35.801
The DNA survived the fire.

01:59:37.103 --> 01:59:37.637
What's more,

01:59:38.037 --> 01:59:43.709
further testing showed the DNA from the victim matched the DNA from the blood in Cruz's car.

01:59:45.611 --> 01:59:51.617
The lab calculated the odds that it wasn't the victim's blood were one in 540 million.

01:59:53.186 --> 01:59:53.486
Dr.

01:59:53.553 --> 01:59:57.723
Sweet proved that Lynn Breeden was in the car the night she died.

02:00:00.226 --> 02:00:02.028
Confronted with the overwhelming evidence,

02:00:02.395 --> 02:00:04.130
Chris Cruz confessed to murder.

02:00:05.264 --> 02:00:06.199
Based on interviews,

02:00:06.532 --> 02:00:10.102
detectives pieced together a likely scenario of that fatal night.

02:00:10.703 --> 02:00:11.170
I'm sorry.

02:00:11.604 --> 02:00:14.640
Breeden once borrowed a great deal of money from Cruz.

02:00:15.641 --> 02:00:16.943
Now he wanted to collect.

02:00:17.944 --> 02:00:19.345
Breeden refused to pay him.

02:00:21.514 --> 02:00:22.782
Cruz lost his temper.

02:00:41.968 --> 02:00:42.268
Growing

02:00:42.335 --> 02:00:42.835
impatient,

02:00:43.502 --> 02:00:44.570
she got out of his car.

02:00:58.718 --> 02:00:59.518
Bruce beat her,

02:01:00.453 --> 02:01:01.687
shoved her body in the trunk,

02:01:02.421 --> 02:01:03.556
and drove to the dumpster.

02:01:30.950 --> 02:01:32.618
When he arrived at the secluded spot,

02:01:33.019 --> 02:01:35.154
the victim began to regain consciousness.

02:01:44.130 --> 02:01:45.498
Chris Cruz shot her.

02:02:25.771 --> 02:02:28.341
He thought the flames would erase all the evidence.

02:02:30.876 --> 02:02:35.614
How could he know the charge of murder would come from his victim's own mouth?

02:02:39.085 --> 02:02:40.853
Found guilty of second degree murder,

02:02:42.154 --> 02:02:44.457
Chris Cruz received a life sentence.

02:02:50.196 --> 02:02:51.530
In rural Clay County,

02:02:51.697 --> 02:02:52.131
Alabama,

02:02:52.598 --> 02:02:57.903
a drive along Route 31 leads to a town caught between memories and progress.

02:03:00.573 --> 02:03:05.511
Where turn-of-the-century storefronts share billing with a new city hall.

02:03:08.280 --> 02:03:10.583
Where folks still get their news word of mouth.

02:03:12.485 --> 02:03:16.055
And where bad news and good news travel at about the same speed.

02:03:18.090 --> 02:03:19.091
Summertime slow.

02:03:23.162 --> 02:03:29.402
It's a safe place where parents don't think twice about giving their teenagers free reign to explore the land.

02:03:30.736 --> 02:03:33.706
A place where violence is an unwelcome stranger.

02:03:40.312 --> 02:03:40.913
On July 22,

02:03:40.913 --> 02:03:42.815
1995,

02:03:43.215 --> 02:03:47.920
three riders on all-terrain vehicles discovered a burned mattress in the woods,

02:03:48.754 --> 02:03:49.422
and beneath it,

02:03:49.822 --> 02:03:51.724
what appeared to be blackened bones.

02:03:56.295 --> 02:03:58.264
I contacted Sheriff Ralph Toland,

02:03:58.531 --> 02:03:59.832
who responded to the call.

02:04:00.666 --> 02:04:01.634
You think you've got a body?

02:04:02.968 --> 02:04:03.502
Three miles.

02:04:03.803 --> 02:04:06.305
Three miles east of 497 .

02:04:06.405 --> 02:04:06.505
OK,

02:04:06.672 --> 02:04:07.306
he's on our way.

02:04:19.018 --> 02:04:22.421
The men met the sheriff and led him to their grim discovery.

02:04:24.957 --> 02:04:26.058
It looked like murder.

02:04:27.126 --> 02:04:29.328
Toland began to secure the crime scene,

02:04:29.762 --> 02:04:31.430
but knew he couldn't do it alone.

02:04:32.264 --> 02:04:35.134
He needed to be sure no evidence was jeopardized.

02:04:35.601 --> 02:04:38.370
I got down into the scene and I saw exactly what had happened.

02:04:38.370 --> 02:04:39.839
I saw the mattress charred and burned,

02:04:40.272 --> 02:04:42.441
nothing left but the springs over a cell,

02:04:42.475 --> 02:04:45.244
and I just stopped everybody where they were at and told them to stop right there.

02:04:45.544 --> 02:04:51.417
Had them to go back out to the entrance from the road and ask them all just to secure it.

02:04:52.151 --> 02:04:56.422
I wanted Perenzeck and the criminalist to come and help us collect this.

02:04:57.189 --> 02:04:57.857
all the evidence.

02:05:00.459 --> 02:05:02.928
The man he wanted was Angelo Dellamanna.

02:05:04.296 --> 02:05:07.533
A scientist with the Alabama Department of Forensic Sciences,

02:05:07.967 --> 02:05:11.737
Dellamanna never knows what he's going to find when he answers a call.

02:05:13.539 --> 02:05:15.140
Whenever you get to a crime scene,

02:05:15.174 --> 02:05:15.374
you hope

02:05:15.374 --> 02:05:18.844
I hope that it's been secure and cordoned off right after the discovery of the body.

02:05:19.278 --> 02:05:21.947
You don't want anybody into the crime scene that doesn't belong there.

02:05:21.947 --> 02:05:23.816
You don't want anybody messing up evidence.

02:05:23.849 --> 02:05:28.921
You want the evidence to be maintained in a secure fashion so that when you as a scientist get there to recover it,

02:05:29.255 --> 02:05:31.023
you can collect it without disturbing it,

02:05:31.457 --> 02:05:32.057
preserve it,

02:05:32.291 --> 02:05:33.626
bring it back to the laboratory,

02:05:33.926 --> 02:05:36.595
and do the test that you need on it to find out who did the crime.

02:05:39.198 --> 02:05:41.300
Della Manna marked the position of the body,

02:05:41.901 --> 02:05:43.135
the condition of the mattress,

02:05:43.269 --> 02:05:47.673
and the surrounding area before he went to work collecting everything he could.

02:05:49.108 --> 02:05:53.512
Della Manna placed flags to mark the position of what little evidence he found.

02:05:54.914 --> 02:05:59.118
He marked 11 used cigarettes randomly scattered around the mattress.

02:06:00.019 --> 02:06:01.253
They hadn't been there long.

02:06:02.154 --> 02:06:03.322
That was a good sign.

02:06:04.223 --> 02:06:05.057
But who smoked them?

02:06:05.558 --> 02:06:06.025
The killer?

02:06:06.759 --> 02:06:07.226
The victim?

02:06:08.093 --> 02:06:11.163
I felt it was going to be very hard not only to identify that person,

02:06:11.263 --> 02:06:12.998
but to generate some kind of evidence.

02:06:13.866 --> 02:06:20.673
I did feel at the crime scene that the cigarette butts present there were going to be very strong evidence because they did not look weathered.

02:06:20.706 --> 02:06:23.108
They were sitting up on top of the cushion of grass.

02:06:23.576 --> 02:06:25.377
I felt they had not been there very long.

02:06:27.179 --> 02:06:29.081
As Delamana assessed the situation,

02:06:29.448 --> 02:06:31.450
his technicians recorded the evidence.

02:06:37.323 --> 02:06:40.759
Since technicians don't want to leave part of themselves behind,

02:06:41.160 --> 02:06:42.127
they wear gloves.

02:06:43.395 --> 02:06:49.602
Delamana's people had been trained so they don't unwittingly destroy the very clues that they're trying to save.

02:06:51.203 --> 02:06:53.906
If forensic scientists are careless in how they collect,

02:06:54.206 --> 02:06:55.808
store or process evidence,

02:06:56.275 --> 02:06:59.211
they'll leave themselves vulnerable to attacks in the courtroom.

02:07:01.480 --> 02:07:02.781
After the evidence was marked,

02:07:03.148 --> 02:07:04.383
photographed and recorded,

02:07:04.917 --> 02:07:06.619
technicians carefully collected it.

02:07:09.722 --> 02:07:12.625
Bodies don't just turn up without someone missing them.

02:07:14.627 --> 02:07:19.098
Sheriff Toland already had a sinking suspicion about who the victim was.

02:07:20.432 --> 02:07:21.300
The week before,

02:07:21.800 --> 02:07:24.370
Jimmy Paris called him to report that his wife,

02:07:24.470 --> 02:07:25.237
Peggy Laughlin,

02:07:25.537 --> 02:07:26.105
was missing.

02:07:28.107 --> 02:07:32.411
Sheriff Toland decided it was time for his deputies to pay Paris a call.

02:07:34.380 --> 02:07:36.315
You can't think of anywhere else you might have gone?

02:07:36.382 --> 02:07:41.053
It was no secret around town that the couple had more than their share of knock-down,

02:07:41.186 --> 02:07:42.154
drag-out fights.

02:07:43.989 --> 02:07:47.259
No one would be surprised if Peggy just decided to up and leave.

02:07:51.163 --> 02:07:51.664
Meanwhile,

02:07:51.930 --> 02:07:57.136
the medical examiner compared the victim's backbone to spinal X-rays of Peggy Laughlin.

02:07:58.470 --> 02:07:59.104
They matched.

02:08:00.239 --> 02:08:02.408
Jimmy Paris became the prime suspect.

02:08:03.409 --> 02:08:04.810
But where was the evidence?

02:08:05.678 --> 02:08:08.714
Paris denied being anywhere near the woods that night.

02:08:12.918 --> 02:08:15.120
Delamana tried to put the pieces together,

02:08:15.721 --> 02:08:18.323
but he didn't have enough of them to complete the picture.

02:08:19.425 --> 02:08:22.394
The forensics team turned up nothing at the crime scene.

02:08:23.228 --> 02:08:26.598
The cigarettes were the only clue untouched by the fire.

02:08:27.700 --> 02:08:29.635
The answer must lie with them.

02:08:30.869 --> 02:08:33.639
Detectives told him both Peggy and Jimmy smoked.

02:08:34.173 --> 02:08:38.811
You can be looking at a case and feel going in that there isn't very much evidence there,

02:08:38.844 --> 02:08:41.313
the investigators don't think there's very much there to work with,

02:08:41.680 --> 02:08:45.017
but when you as a forensic scientist find that small piece of evidence,

02:08:45.017 --> 02:08:50.255
that one link that can either nail the suspect or clear a falsely accused individual,

02:08:50.589 --> 02:08:53.525
the rush that comes from that makes all the long hours worthwhile.

02:08:55.394 --> 02:08:58.097
If he could lift DNA from the cigarette filters,

02:08:58.363 --> 02:09:01.533
he could compare it to the DNA of Paris and Laughlin.

02:09:02.835 --> 02:09:04.937
If he could prove they were together in the woods,

02:09:05.304 --> 02:09:07.506
Toland could make a case against Paris.

02:09:09.208 --> 02:09:12.177
Dellamanna shined an ultraviolet light on the cigarettes.

02:09:13.212 --> 02:09:15.614
The light revealed the saliva-soaked areas.

02:09:16.582 --> 02:09:19.818
DNA can be obtained from saliva and lip cells.

02:09:21.053 --> 02:09:24.623
He marked the outline of the glowing portion he wanted to test.

02:09:28.327 --> 02:09:33.432
Then he carefully trimmed it away and sent it to the lab for DNA profiling.

02:09:34.099 --> 02:09:35.567
To build a DNA profile,

02:09:35.601 --> 02:09:37.903
what we do first is we take our evidence sample,

02:09:38.237 --> 02:09:40.906
blood or saliva or whatever the DNA is in.

02:09:42.274 --> 02:09:45.277
What we do is we break open the cell and release the DNA.

02:09:45.711 --> 02:09:47.946
So we extract the DNA from whatever it's on.

02:09:48.580 --> 02:09:52.651
We clean it up and we look at specific areas or addresses on the DNA.

02:09:52.951 --> 02:09:57.089
And we make millions of copies of that area big enough so that we can see it.

02:09:57.823 --> 02:10:01.059
Once the DNA profile is done for each person in the case,

02:10:01.059 --> 02:10:03.729
we can then compare it to the evidence DNA profile.

02:10:03.996 --> 02:10:06.098
and see if there's any matches there at all.

02:10:08.600 --> 02:10:10.302
He was encouraged by the results.

02:10:10.903 --> 02:10:13.572
The lab identified DNA from two people,

02:10:14.439 --> 02:10:17.342
and two of the cigarettes held DNA from both people,

02:10:17.843 --> 02:10:19.511
meaning they shared their smokes.

02:10:20.679 --> 02:10:25.984
Could Delamanna prove those two people were Jimmy Paris and Peggy Laughlin?

02:10:31.456 --> 02:10:33.625
Testing Paris's DNA was simple.

02:10:34.526 --> 02:10:35.661
Cooperating with police,

02:10:35.861 --> 02:10:37.062
he gave a blood sample,

02:10:37.429 --> 02:10:40.032
which provided ample DNA to analyze.

02:10:45.704 --> 02:10:48.707
But getting Laughlin's DNA proved much more difficult.

02:10:50.776 --> 02:10:53.745
Delamanna had to try extracting it from her remains.

02:10:55.080 --> 02:10:59.218
He felt the best place to look for the DNA was in the marrow of the femur,

02:10:59.551 --> 02:11:00.385
or thigh bone.

02:11:01.386 --> 02:11:02.821
The largest bone in the body,

02:11:03.255 --> 02:11:06.758
it's the one most likely to have protected DNA from the fire.

02:11:08.994 --> 02:11:10.929
Bone marrow is rich in DNA.

02:11:12.030 --> 02:11:14.333
To expose as many cells as possible,

02:11:14.733 --> 02:11:16.635
Delamana ground it into a powder.

02:11:18.470 --> 02:11:22.007
He gave the powder to another scientist for DNA extraction.

02:11:26.478 --> 02:11:31.550
The scientist places the powder into a chemical bath that breaks open the cell's nuclei,

02:11:32.017 --> 02:11:33.018
releasing the DNA.

02:11:39.091 --> 02:11:40.893
Once the DNA is in the solution,

02:11:41.393 --> 02:11:42.160
bone fragments,

02:11:42.394 --> 02:11:42.961
chemicals,

02:11:43.028 --> 02:11:44.997
and impurities must be removed.

02:11:48.267 --> 02:11:51.103
The liquid goes through a series of microscopic filters.

02:11:54.306 --> 02:11:56.575
A centrifuge spins away the refuse.

02:11:57.342 --> 02:11:59.444
The filters trap the clean DNA.

02:12:01.880 --> 02:12:02.514
Theoretically,

02:12:02.748 --> 02:12:05.817
scientists can obtain DNA from just one cell.

02:12:06.818 --> 02:12:08.153
But to create a profile,

02:12:08.487 --> 02:12:10.088
they generally need thousands,

02:12:10.422 --> 02:12:11.823
though that number is decreasing.

02:12:13.025 --> 02:12:13.725
Not long ago,

02:12:14.293 --> 02:12:16.728
tests required a sample the size of a dime.

02:12:17.663 --> 02:12:18.030
Today,

02:12:18.363 --> 02:12:22.834
they can extract usable DNA from a drop of blood the size of a pinhead.

02:12:25.070 --> 02:12:26.705
The DNA is isolated.

02:12:27.539 --> 02:12:29.041
Now it's time to measure it.

02:12:34.746 --> 02:12:36.949
When the DNA is drawn through a membrane,

02:12:37.382 --> 02:12:38.884
it creates a band of color.

02:12:41.887 --> 02:12:43.088
By comparing the color,

02:12:43.689 --> 02:12:46.692
scientists can determine how much DNA they have.

02:13:01.006 --> 02:13:02.841
Delamana examines the results.

02:13:03.342 --> 02:13:06.678
which appear as small dark bands on a piece of film.

02:13:07.980 --> 02:13:11.650
DNA is measured in minuscule quantities called nanograms,

02:13:11.917 --> 02:13:13.352
or billionths of a gram.

02:13:14.386 --> 02:13:18.724
The test results tell Dallamana whether he has enough DNA to work with.

02:13:19.491 --> 02:13:20.225
Unfortunately,

02:13:20.359 --> 02:13:23.895
there's only about 0.3 nanograms of DNA in that sample.

02:13:25.030 --> 02:13:30.669
That's 0.903 grams of DNA in that sample.

02:13:32.304 --> 02:13:36.141
Delamana didn't have enough usable DNA for testing.

02:13:37.075 --> 02:13:38.944
But he had an ace up his sleeve.

02:13:39.678 --> 02:13:42.147
A scientific process so revolutionary,

02:13:42.514 --> 02:13:43.115
its inventor,

02:13:43.415 --> 02:13:43.815
Kerry B.

02:13:43.949 --> 02:13:44.316
Mullis,

02:13:44.649 --> 02:13:46.118
won the Nobel Prize.

02:13:53.191 --> 02:13:54.960
Up until 1993,

02:13:55.427 --> 02:13:59.297
scientists could test DNA only if they had large quantities.

02:14:00.132 --> 02:14:00.966
But bacteria,

02:14:01.333 --> 02:14:03.335
heat and time take their toll,

02:14:04.002 --> 02:14:06.872
degrading DNA and leaving it in fragments.

02:14:08.373 --> 02:14:10.242
A process called PCR,

02:14:10.742 --> 02:14:12.577
or polymerase chain reaction,

02:14:13.178 --> 02:14:16.214
enables scientists to make multiple copies of DNA.

02:14:19.718 --> 02:14:22.487
DNA is made up of four chemical units.

02:14:22.954 --> 02:14:23.989
Adenine ,

02:14:24.489 --> 02:14:25.490
Thymine ,

02:14:25.891 --> 02:14:27.959
Guanine and Cytosine .

02:14:29.327 --> 02:14:31.730
These units are strung together like beads.

02:14:32.664 --> 02:14:33.565
A joins B and C.

02:14:33.832 --> 02:14:37.402
only with T and G joins only with C.

02:14:39.438 --> 02:14:44.443
PCR works by splitting apart the double stranded DNA like a zipper.

02:14:46.211 --> 02:14:52.417
The thermocycler heats the DNA separating the A from the T and the G from the C,

02:14:53.518 --> 02:14:56.088
essentially unzipping the helix down the center.

02:14:57.589 --> 02:14:59.925
More of these chemicals are added to the solution.

02:15:00.625 --> 02:15:04.029
And the two halves of the helix use them to make themselves whole.

02:15:04.996 --> 02:15:07.799
Repeat the process and the two become four.

02:15:08.567 --> 02:15:13.305
And so on until millions of copies are created and the sample can be measured.

02:15:15.207 --> 02:15:16.942
When making a DNA profile,

02:15:17.242 --> 02:15:20.946
scientists don't need to compare the entire DNA molecule.

02:15:21.780 --> 02:15:24.349
They only look at several sites along the helix.

02:15:25.550 --> 02:15:27.953
If these sites are the same on both samples,

02:15:28.253 --> 02:15:30.956
the DNA most likely came from the same person.

02:15:31.756 --> 02:15:33.091
It's considered a match.

02:15:34.426 --> 02:15:39.965
Scientists used PCR to create enough DNA from the bone to do a profile.

02:15:40.799 --> 02:15:43.568
But the quantity and quality of the DNA was poor,

02:15:44.035 --> 02:15:45.904
so they could measure only one site.

02:15:46.972 --> 02:15:48.173
But in order to make a match,

02:15:48.507 --> 02:15:50.775
they needed to identify at least two sites.

02:15:51.910 --> 02:15:54.112
How could they find that second site?

02:15:55.113 --> 02:15:59.584
Delamanna knew that a child's DNA is a combination of both parents'

02:15:59.684 --> 02:16:00.085
DNA.

02:16:01.186 --> 02:16:02.954
To solve Peggy Laughlin's murder,

02:16:03.388 --> 02:16:06.191
Delamanna needed help from her brothers and sisters.

02:16:07.058 --> 02:16:08.493
If he could do their profiles,

02:16:08.827 --> 02:16:11.596
then he could work backward and calculate Peggy's.

02:16:12.130 --> 02:16:17.135
Once we knew we didn't have enough DNA to build Peggy's complete DNA profile,

02:16:17.669 --> 02:16:23.041
we had to go and get a blood sample from every one of her brothers and sisters that were in the United States.

02:16:23.175 --> 02:16:25.644
These samples came from all over the United States.

02:16:27.679 --> 02:16:30.048
As Delamana's investigation continued,

02:16:30.482 --> 02:16:35.187
Sheriff Toland received a call about an abandoned van found in a neighboring town.

02:16:36.788 --> 02:16:39.558
A license check showed it belonged to Jimmy Paris.

02:16:42.227 --> 02:16:42.794
Inside,

02:16:43.094 --> 02:16:46.331
forensic scientists found blood on the ceiling and walls.

02:16:47.832 --> 02:16:49.534
Part of the carpeting had been torn out.

02:16:52.938 --> 02:16:53.438
I'll tell you what,

02:16:53.471 --> 02:16:53.572
man.

02:16:53.572 --> 02:16:54.573
Look above your head.

02:16:54.973 --> 02:16:55.640
Right above your head.

02:16:55.840 --> 02:16:56.174
Whoa,

02:16:56.341 --> 02:16:56.508
yeah.

02:16:56.508 --> 02:16:57.175
You got three.

02:16:57.175 --> 02:16:57.576
I see it.

02:16:59.744 --> 02:17:00.779
Based on this evidence,

02:17:01.079 --> 02:17:03.114
they obtained a search warrant for Paris'

02:17:03.215 --> 02:17:03.481
home.

02:17:04.015 --> 02:17:04.149
Hey,

02:17:04.149 --> 02:17:04.749
Jimmy.

02:17:04.749 --> 02:17:04.816
Hey.

02:17:04.849 --> 02:17:05.483
Sorry to barge in.

02:17:05.483 --> 02:17:06.384
Let us in.

02:17:06.384 --> 02:17:07.619
That'll help us just fine.

02:17:07.852 --> 02:17:08.587
Stand side there.

02:17:08.620 --> 02:17:08.887
Back off.

02:17:08.887 --> 02:17:10.388
We've got a phone here.

02:17:14.993 --> 02:17:19.531
Deputies searched the house for any clues that could link Paris to his wife's death.

02:17:22.167 --> 02:17:23.168
They found nothing.

02:17:33.912 --> 02:17:39.184
Then an officer checked the crawl space and found a section of blood-soaked carpet.

02:17:40.185 --> 02:17:42.420
It matched the missing piece from Paris'

02:17:42.487 --> 02:17:44.589
van like a jigsaw puzzle.

02:17:46.091 --> 02:17:49.527
The possession of the carpet linked Paris to the bloodied van.

02:17:50.729 --> 02:17:52.097
Police had the murder scene,

02:17:52.564 --> 02:17:53.331
they had the victim,

02:17:53.732 --> 02:17:55.066
and they had their suspect.

02:17:56.167 --> 02:18:00.338
The evidence was strong enough to arrest Jimmy Paris for the murder of his wife.

02:18:01.473 --> 02:18:03.441
But to strengthen their case for conviction,

02:18:04.142 --> 02:18:05.677
police needed to link Paris,

02:18:06.211 --> 02:18:06.745
the van,

02:18:07.112 --> 02:18:08.813
and the burned mattress together.

02:18:09.781 --> 02:18:10.382
To do that,

02:18:10.815 --> 02:18:14.319
Paris had to be placed with the victim just before her death.

02:18:15.420 --> 02:18:19.324
Angelo Della Manna was hard at work trying to accomplish just that.

02:18:20.225 --> 02:18:25.363
He was ready to make Peggy's DNA profile using the blood samples from her siblings.

02:18:26.331 --> 02:18:27.432
We extracted them,

02:18:27.766 --> 02:18:30.235
put them through our full battery of DNA tests,

02:18:30.335 --> 02:18:34.005
and what we did was when we got the DNA profiles of the brothers and the sisters,

02:18:34.306 --> 02:18:39.511
we were able to work backwards and build the rest of Peggy's profile.

02:18:41.279 --> 02:18:47.986
The genetic information Delamana gathered from the siblings enabled him to complete the missing link on Peggy's DNA.

02:18:50.221 --> 02:18:53.124
Peggy's DNA matched the DNA on the cigarettes.

02:18:53.958 --> 02:18:58.697
Here was proof that Peggy was alive with Jimmy in the woods the night she died.

02:19:00.131 --> 02:19:02.000
For forensics expert Paul Ferrara,

02:19:02.400 --> 02:19:07.105
the DNA match was the kind of evidence that tipped the scales against Jimmy Paris.

02:19:07.339 --> 02:19:12.277
When looking at DNA evidence and the weight that you put on that DNA evidence,

02:19:13.511 --> 02:19:20.952
take that and place it in perspective with other evidence of motive,

02:19:21.186 --> 02:19:21.519
means,

02:19:21.553 --> 02:19:22.387
and opportunity.

02:19:23.555 --> 02:19:25.457
And when you combine all of those together,

02:19:26.057 --> 02:19:33.398
then one can come to an informed opinion that a person is guilty beyond reasonable doubt.

02:19:42.240 --> 02:19:43.341
Harris had the motive,

02:19:43.708 --> 02:19:44.142
means,

02:19:44.242 --> 02:19:46.578
and opportunity to kill Peggy Laughlin.

02:19:49.314 --> 02:19:50.415
He was a jealous man.

02:19:51.783 --> 02:19:54.018
and he'd begun to suspect Peggy of infidelity.

02:19:55.854 --> 02:19:56.454
On that night,

02:19:56.688 --> 02:19:58.289
he invited her into the woods.

02:19:59.157 --> 02:20:00.358
He brought along a mattress,

02:20:00.892 --> 02:20:03.361
placing it on the ground and covering it with a blanket.

02:20:10.835 --> 02:20:12.804
Peggy and Jimmy smoked and talked,

02:20:13.705 --> 02:20:16.241
casually tossing aside the spent cigarettes.

02:20:28.853 --> 02:20:29.154
There,

02:20:29.521 --> 02:20:31.322
in the saliva and lip cells,

02:20:31.656 --> 02:20:33.825
lay the code of the murderer's identity,

02:20:34.426 --> 02:20:36.127
waiting to reveal its secret.

02:20:37.362 --> 02:20:37.595
Then,

02:20:37.962 --> 02:20:42.734
something happened that triggered a rage that drove Jimmy Paris to homicide.

02:21:55.173 --> 02:21:59.944
Couldn't see that the evidence he had already left would be enough to convict him.

02:22:01.846 --> 02:22:07.151
DNA analysis was crucial in the sense that it placed both the suspect and victim at the scene together.

02:22:08.186 --> 02:22:09.053
And in that case...

02:22:09.721 --> 02:22:19.197
Having those small pieces of evidence allowed us to put a guilty man in prison and allow the family to put closure to a terrible incident.

02:22:21.533 --> 02:22:23.401
In Alabama and Vancouver,

02:22:23.935 --> 02:22:28.806
scientists struggle to find DNA evidence to link a killer to his victim.

02:22:29.908 --> 02:22:34.879
But the power of DNA is not just its ability to put people behind bars.

02:22:35.780 --> 02:22:38.883
It also holds the key to setting them free.

02:22:46.457 --> 02:22:47.625
Attorney Barry Sheck,

02:22:47.992 --> 02:22:48.593
part of O.J.

02:22:48.860 --> 02:22:50.295
Simpson's criminal defense team,

02:22:50.762 --> 02:22:53.598
doesn't believe everyone behind bars is guilty.

02:22:54.732 --> 02:22:55.533
Using DNA,

02:22:56.100 --> 02:22:57.735
he's making a strong case.

02:22:59.170 --> 02:23:01.005
Sheck heads the Innocence Project,

02:23:01.306 --> 02:23:02.840
a program at the Benjamin N.

02:23:02.941 --> 02:23:05.043
Cardozo School of Law in New York.

02:23:06.377 --> 02:23:10.949
The program is dedicated to exonerating the innocent through DNA profiling.

02:23:11.015 --> 02:23:12.383
...Cereological Institute...

02:23:14.152 --> 02:23:16.187
...and that they had actually responded,

02:23:16.187 --> 02:23:18.122
we have something but not enough to test.

02:23:18.389 --> 02:23:20.658
Scheck provides the expertise.

02:23:21.392 --> 02:23:22.727
The students do the legwork,

02:23:23.261 --> 02:23:24.862
sifting through hundreds of cases,

02:23:25.296 --> 02:23:27.131
looking for those with the most promise.

02:23:29.367 --> 02:23:30.735
Once cases are identified,

02:23:31.035 --> 02:23:34.072
the students investigate whether the evidence still exists.

02:23:34.906 --> 02:23:35.540
If it does,

02:23:35.873 --> 02:23:38.476
they see if it can be used for DNA testing.

02:23:39.344 --> 02:23:39.711
I don't know.

02:23:39.844 --> 02:23:42.046
There's nothing in the Redwell 2 yet.

02:23:42.280 --> 02:23:44.616
It became clear to us that

02:23:45.083 --> 02:23:48.586
DNA had the power to go back into old cases,

02:23:49.954 --> 02:23:50.688
re-examine it,

02:23:51.222 --> 02:23:57.829
and exonerate people that had been convicted many years ago that lots of people felt might be innocent.

02:23:59.230 --> 02:24:07.372
The Innocence Project helped exonerate 30 of the 40 people released through DNA since the project began in 1992.

02:24:08.706 --> 02:24:09.073
Today,

02:24:09.574 --> 02:24:17.248
startling FBI statistics show that one-quarter to one-third of all DNA testing at its lab clears a suspect.

02:24:19.250 --> 02:24:23.788
No one knows how many innocent people remain in prison across the United States,

02:24:24.322 --> 02:24:27.725
but the Innocence Project is doing its best to find them.

02:24:28.626 --> 02:24:34.065
Now we have all these cases of people who have been convicted who can credibly come...

02:24:34.165 --> 02:24:34.766
forward and say,

02:24:34.866 --> 02:24:35.066
look,

02:24:35.767 --> 02:24:36.968
I didn't commit this crime.

02:24:37.502 --> 02:24:39.904
There was serological evidence that was introduced against me.

02:24:40.104 --> 02:24:42.206
There was hair evidence that was introduced against me,

02:24:42.273 --> 02:24:42.874
for example.

02:24:44.208 --> 02:24:47.111
Please test items in my case.

02:24:47.512 --> 02:24:48.513
It'll prove me innocent.

02:24:49.113 --> 02:24:52.183
And that's happening in case after case after case.

02:24:55.119 --> 02:24:58.723
Louis Hochberg is the executive director of the Innocence Project.

02:24:58.723 --> 02:25:00.625
In 1993,

02:25:01.025 --> 02:25:04.862
a convict named Troy Webb appealed to her colleagues for help.

02:25:05.763 --> 02:25:09.233
He trusted DNA to prove he was innocent of rape.

02:25:10.001 --> 02:25:12.637
Troy Webb first wrote to us in 1993,

02:25:12.637 --> 02:25:17.275
and I understand from Troy that the way he got to us was through his cellmate,

02:25:17.709 --> 02:25:20.745
who apparently was also a client of the Innocence Project.

02:25:22.814 --> 02:25:27.652
Webb was arrested in 1988 after a rape victim picked him out of a photo lineup.

02:25:28.853 --> 02:25:35.560
Eyewitness identification has led to most convictions where defendants were later proved innocent through DNA profiling.

02:25:37.729 --> 02:25:39.897
Hochberg believed in Webb's innocence,

02:25:40.264 --> 02:25:45.636
but it would be three years before she and her students returned him to his family in Virginia Beach,

02:25:45.703 --> 02:25:46.170
Virginia.

02:25:46.704 --> 02:25:51.008
The principal criteria for us is number one.

02:25:52.143 --> 02:25:54.278
can this person make a credible claim of innocence?

02:25:54.846 --> 02:25:55.613
And number two,

02:25:55.680 --> 02:25:56.547
the most important,

02:25:57.482 --> 02:26:00.585
is there biological evidence in the case that we can test,

02:26:01.285 --> 02:26:05.890
which combined with other factors will be determinative,

02:26:06.157 --> 02:26:11.796
will prove that this person did not commit the crime for which he or she was convicted?

02:26:13.331 --> 02:26:18.536
The Innocence Project had evidence in the case sent to Paul Ferrara's forensic lab in Richmond.

02:26:21.439 --> 02:26:21.706
There,

02:26:22.006 --> 02:26:28.646
scientists compared DNA from evidence left at the rape scene to DNA in a blood sample from Troy Webb.

02:26:29.080 --> 02:26:31.149
The level of the technology,

02:26:32.016 --> 02:26:33.351
even 10 years ago,

02:26:34.051 --> 02:26:37.188
was such that we couldn't eliminate

02:26:37.855 --> 02:26:38.489
Troy Webb,

02:26:38.556 --> 02:26:39.290
for example,

02:26:39.357 --> 02:26:42.493
as the possible contributor of the genetic material.

02:26:43.261 --> 02:26:44.362
But by the same token,

02:26:44.462 --> 02:26:47.832
we wouldn't have been able to eliminate a large percent of the population.

02:26:51.035 --> 02:26:53.070
Before they could start the testing process,

02:26:53.404 --> 02:26:58.176
the lab had to retrieve genetic material from evidence stored seven years earlier.

02:27:01.479 --> 02:27:02.580
Once it was identified,

02:27:02.814 --> 02:27:05.283
they had to see if it contained viable DNA.

02:27:10.888 --> 02:27:15.026
They used an acid test to confirm the biological fluid was present.

02:27:16.894 --> 02:27:18.162
If the fluid is present,

02:27:18.496 --> 02:27:20.498
the indicator liquid turns purple.

02:27:23.935 --> 02:27:29.340
Another method is to use a light source called an omnicrome that makes fluids glow.

02:27:34.745 --> 02:27:37.682
If the scientist is looking for blood to test for DNA,

02:27:38.282 --> 02:27:40.818
she'll use a chemical called luminol to find it.

02:27:42.086 --> 02:27:44.689
Luminol can detect the faintest trace of blood,

02:27:45.256 --> 02:27:52.230
making it especially helpful in instances where blood may have been washed away by rain or where a killer has tried to remove it.

02:27:53.831 --> 02:27:55.032
In Troy Webb's case,

02:27:55.333 --> 02:27:58.269
investigators swapped the evidence from the victim's clothing.

02:28:00.805 --> 02:28:03.274
The sample would contain the perpetrator's DNA.

02:28:05.343 --> 02:28:07.445
They'll compare it with Troy's DNA.

02:28:10.114 --> 02:28:17.421
Comparing DNA is a matter of comparing the lengths of strands of genetic code at specific points on the double helix.

02:28:19.323 --> 02:28:23.494
Scientists place the DNA sections they want to compare into a gel.

02:28:24.695 --> 02:28:26.764
The DNA has a negative charge,

02:28:27.098 --> 02:28:30.701
so scientists can use a positive charge to pull it through the gel.

02:28:32.803 --> 02:28:36.374
The longer pieces travel more slowly than the shorter pieces.

02:28:37.408 --> 02:28:41.045
So two pieces of the same length would move the same distance.

02:28:44.248 --> 02:28:49.453
If the DNA from the evidence and the suspect move the same distance across the gel,

02:28:50.254 --> 02:28:53.824
scientists know they're the same length and they have a match.

02:28:55.126 --> 02:28:57.295
If the segments don't move the same distance,

02:28:57.728 --> 02:28:59.764
they couldn't belong to the same person.

02:29:04.101 --> 02:29:09.440
The segment lengths can be seen and measured under lights that make the DNA fluoresce.

02:29:14.512 --> 02:29:19.150
These are the printouts of the actual DNA profiles in the Troy Webb case.

02:29:21.352 --> 02:29:22.553
They fade with time,

02:29:22.853 --> 02:29:25.790
so scientists take photographs for a permanent record.

02:29:29.360 --> 02:29:35.967
Scientists needed to compare only one site on Troy Webb's DNA strand to the DNA from the evidence.

02:29:36.701 --> 02:29:37.601
If it didn't match,

02:29:38.069 --> 02:29:39.804
he couldn't have committed the crime.

02:29:41.238 --> 02:29:41.906
4.2,

02:29:42.006 --> 02:29:42.673
4.3,

02:29:42.907 --> 02:29:43.941
and the corresponding pattern.

02:29:44.875 --> 02:29:47.678
Besides comparing Webb's DNA to the evidence,

02:29:48.446 --> 02:29:52.149
it's also compared to the DNA of the victim and the rapist.

02:29:55.152 --> 02:29:59.590
The dot patterns make the differences between the DNA perfectly clear.

02:30:02.360 --> 02:30:05.696
None of the DNA matches the DNA from the evidence.

02:30:06.497 --> 02:30:08.666
This DNA type did not come from Troy Webb,

02:30:09.166 --> 02:30:12.069
and the true rapist in this case is actually still at large right now.

02:30:14.138 --> 02:30:15.106
On September 7,

02:30:15.106 --> 02:30:16.607
1996,

02:30:17.008 --> 02:30:19.410
the Innocence Project released the results.

02:30:21.078 --> 02:30:21.712
In Virginia,

02:30:22.113 --> 02:30:26.484
where new evidence may not be presented more than 21 days after sentencing,

02:30:26.951 --> 02:30:28.753
the only option was clemency.

02:30:30.021 --> 02:30:33.157
The governor reviewed the petition and granted the pardon.

02:30:33.991 --> 02:30:37.495
There is no greater good that a lawyer can do,

02:30:38.295 --> 02:30:39.163
in my judgment,

02:30:39.730 --> 02:30:42.800
than to look back at old cases,

02:30:43.534 --> 02:30:46.871
and if it can be demonstrated that an injustice was done,

02:30:47.772 --> 02:30:48.305
to write it.

02:30:50.674 --> 02:30:51.575
On October 16th,

02:30:51.575 --> 02:30:52.843
1996,

02:30:53.711 --> 02:30:56.814
Troy Webb emerged from prison a free man.

02:30:57.748 --> 02:31:02.920
The first thing he wanted to do was eat a seven-foot sub from Philadelphia cold cuts.

02:31:05.756 --> 02:31:07.258
He now works at the sub shop.

02:31:09.193 --> 02:31:12.496
He's made new friends and reestablished a bond with family.

02:31:13.497 --> 02:31:17.368
He lives with the same faith and hope that kept him going in prison.

02:31:18.202 --> 02:31:20.404
And he's grateful to the Innocence Project.

02:31:21.238 --> 02:31:22.039
I like family,

02:31:22.139 --> 02:31:22.406
really,

02:31:22.406 --> 02:31:22.640
you know,

02:31:22.673 --> 02:31:23.441
because if it wasn't for them,

02:31:23.507 --> 02:31:24.708
I'd probably still be locked up.

02:31:26.677 --> 02:31:27.344
But they helped me,

02:31:27.445 --> 02:31:27.745
you know,

02:31:27.845 --> 02:31:28.712
for free at that,

02:31:28.746 --> 02:31:29.046
you know.

02:31:29.647 --> 02:31:29.780
So,

02:31:30.614 --> 02:31:31.782
couldn't ask for too much more.

02:31:35.553 --> 02:31:38.622
All hope is not lost of catching the real rapist.

02:31:39.557 --> 02:31:46.597
Virginia has amassed the largest database of DNA in the country by collecting blood samples from every convicted felon.

02:31:47.798 --> 02:31:51.035
Tens of thousands of blood samples pour in every year,

02:31:51.735 --> 02:31:56.874
creating a backlog of more than 135,000 samples in cold storage.

02:31:58.409 --> 02:31:58.709
Still,

02:31:59.076 --> 02:32:02.513
of the nearly 10,000 DNA profiles in the data bank,

02:32:03.080 --> 02:32:08.519
Virginia has already solved 18 crimes by matching DNA from the crime scene.

02:32:10.421 --> 02:32:14.325
If the DNA from the case can be matched with DNA in the data bank,

02:32:15.426 --> 02:32:16.994
the rapist will be found.

02:32:17.795 --> 02:32:21.365
Our DNA data bank here was one of the early ones created.

02:32:21.665 --> 02:32:24.235
I think we had one hit in 1993,

02:32:24.235 --> 02:32:25.936
none in 1994,

02:32:26.504 --> 02:32:29.206
and then five in 1995,

02:32:29.406 --> 02:32:34.078
then 12 in 1996 alone.

02:32:34.812 --> 02:32:36.180
And the database,

02:32:36.580 --> 02:32:38.516
even in 1996,

02:32:39.216 --> 02:32:46.557
only numbered less than 9,000 profiles out of those 130,000 samples we have.

02:32:47.358 --> 02:32:47.558
When?

02:32:47.758 --> 02:32:51.862
we get to the point where we have the convicted felon profiles,

02:32:52.263 --> 02:32:54.098
all 130,000 of them.

02:32:54.765 --> 02:33:02.106
The number of these hits is going to skyrocket dramatically until the point at which they are commonplace.

02:33:03.607 --> 02:33:05.809
If you are innocent of a crime,

02:33:06.477 --> 02:33:10.147
DNA technology is your best friend,

02:33:10.181 --> 02:33:10.948
your best ally.

02:33:11.782 --> 02:33:12.650
But conversely,

02:33:12.750 --> 02:33:13.684
if you're guilty,

02:33:13.918 --> 02:33:14.785
it's your worst enemy.

02:33:17.688 --> 02:33:22.793
DNA profiling holds tremendous power for a technology still in its infancy.

02:33:24.061 --> 02:33:25.429
In its first 10 years,

02:33:25.763 --> 02:33:31.101
the diminutive double helix has quickly become one of the new detective's biggest allies.

02:34:27.424 --> 02:34:28.492
Thank you.

